// Generated from refs/logo/Berkeley-Logo-Reference-Manual.html, then edited by hand.
// Phases (the `phase` node on each command):
//   1  Turtle drawing: turtle and pen commands, procedures and recursion, variables,
//      REPEAT / IF / STOP / OUTPUT, arithmetic, printing, and the few list commands
//      needed for POS / SETPOS.
//   2  Complete core language and filled shapes: the rest of words, lists and arrays,
//      loops, CATCH / THROW, MAP / FILTER / FOREACH and friends, SETFILLCOLOR and
//      the palette.
//   3  Performance: metrics and regression checks; no new commands.
//   4  Watching a program run: repeat counter and variable panels; no new commands.
//   5  Help panel, examples and tutorial: no new commands (see implementation-plan.md).
//   6  Interactive programs: mouse, keyboard and console input, BUTTONACT / KEYACT.
//   7  Advanced and niche: fills, labels and pen modes, objects, macros, property
//      lists, file I/O, SHELL, bitwise operations, introspection / metaprogramming
//      and obscure library procedures.
//   #null  Obsolete: not planned. obsolete_reason says why, after "EDIT isn't in Pendown: ".
// Our changes to UCBLogo (see "Dialect and extensions" in implementation-plan.md):
//   extension #true  A command that isn't in the manual; `source` says where the idea came from.
//   differs "..."    How our version behaves differently from the manual's `desc`.
// The help panel (phase 5), for implemented commands and the syntax entries:
//   help "..."       Our own description, for our dialect, in the panel's markup
//                    (crates/pendown/src/markup.rs). `desc` stays as the manual's.
//   example "..."    A short runnable program, any number, with title="..." and,
//                    for one that stops with an error on purpose, fails=#true.
//                    They're tests: crates/pendown/tests/examples.rs.
//   see "name" ...   More for "See also" than the rest of the section.
//   names "..." ...  More names the help panel finds it by, such as "#".
// Syntax that isn't a command in the manual (it's in the manual's chapter 1,
// which this file leaves out), for the help panel. `names` are what the
// editor's cursor can be on.
syntax {
  numbers {
    title "Numbers"
    eg "100  -45  3.5  1e3"
    help """
    Numbers can be whole, `100`, have a fraction, `3.5`, or be negative, `-45`. Very big or small numbers can be written with an exponent: `1e3` is 1000. A number prints the way it was typed until it's worked out, so `PRINT 1e3` prints `1e3`, but `PRINT 1e3 + 1` prints `1001`.

    A number is a word, so it can be used wherever a word can, and it doesn't need a quote: `PRINT 42` and `PRINT "42` print the same. Answers are worked out to about 15 digits.
    """
    example title="Kinds of number" """
    PRINT 100
    PRINT -45 + 3.5
    PRINT 1e3 + 1
    PRINT 2 / 3
    """
    see "sum" "numberp" "form"
  }
  quoted-words {
    title "Quoted words: \"word"
    eg "\"word"
    names "\""
    help """
    A quote in front of a word, `"red`, means the word itself, rather than a command or procedure to run. It's how you give a name to [MAKE], a colour to [SETPENCOLOR] (see [Colours]), or a word to [PRINT].

    The quote goes only at the front: there's no closing one. A word ends at a space or a bracket.
    """
    example title="Words, not commands" """
    PRINT "hello
    SETPC "orange
    MAKE "size 120
    REPEAT 3 [FD :size RT 120]
    """
    see "variables" "lists" "word"
  }
  colours {
    title "Colours"
    names "COLOURS" "COLORS" "COLOUR" "COLOR"
    help """
    Every command that takes a colour, such as [SETPENCOLOR], [SETBACKGROUND] or [SETFILLCOLOR], takes it in any of the forms below. Find a colour here, and copy it into your program at the cursor.
    """
    example title="Colours in four forms" """
    SETBG "midnightblue
    SETPC "#ff9a26
    SETFC [255 99 71 128]
    CIRCLE 100
    """
    see "setpencolor" "setbackground" "setfillcolor" "setpalette" "palette"
  }
  variables {
    title "Variables: :name"
    eg ":name"
    names ":"
    help """
    A colon in front of a name, `:size`, gives the value of the variable with that name. Variables are made by [MAKE], and by a procedure's inputs, as in `TO SQUARE.ONE :size`.

    The variables panel shows every variable the running instruction can see, as Kame goes.
    """
    example title="A variable for a size" """
    MAKE "size 50
    REPEAT 6 [
      REPEAT 4 [FD :size RT 90]
      MAKE "size :size + 20
    ]
    """
    see "make" "thing" "local"
  }
  lists {
    title "Lists: [ ]"
    eg "[thing1 thing2 ...]"
    names "[" "]"
    help """
    Square brackets make a list: `[red green blue]`. Words inside are taken as they're written, so they don't need quotes, and nothing is worked out: `[1 + 2]` is a list of three words.

    Lists of instructions are how [REPEAT], [IF] and the other control commands are given what to run. Lists can hold lists.
    """
    example title="Data and instructions" """
    SHOW [red green blue]
    SHOW [1 + 2]
    REPEAT 3 [FD 100 RT 120]
    """
    see "list" "sentence" "first" "run"
  }
  arrays {
    title "Arrays: { }"
    eg """
    {thing1 thing2 ...}
    {thing1 thing2 ...}@origin
    """
    names "{" "}"
    help """
    Braces make an array: `{10 20 30}`. Like a list, but its members can be changed, with [SETITEM]. An array counts from 1, or from the number after an `@`: `{a b c}@0` counts from 0.
    """
    example title="An array to change" """
    MAKE "scores {0 0 0}
    SETITEM 2 :scores 5
    SHOW :scores
    SHOW ITEM 0 {a b c}@0
    """
    see "array" "setitem" "item"
  }
  parentheses {
    title "Parentheses: ( )"
    eg """
    (expression)
    (PROCNAME input1 input2 input3 ...)
    """
    names "(" ")"
    help """
    Parentheses do two things. Around a calculation, they say what to work out first: `(2 + 3) * 4` is 20.

    Around a command and its inputs, they let it take more or fewer inputs than usual, as in `(PRINT "a "b "c)` or `(SUM 1 2 3 4)`.
    """
    example title="Working out first" """
    PRINT 2 + 3 * 4
    PRINT (2 + 3) * 4
    """
    example title="More inputs" """
    (PRINT "Kame "likes "squares)
    PRINT (SUM 1 2 3 4)
    """
    see "operators" "print"
  }
  operators {
    title "Operators: + - * / = < >"
    eg """
    num1 + num2    num1 - num2    num1 * num2    num1 / num2
    thing1 = thing2    thing1 <> thing2
    num1 < num2    num1 > num2    num1 <= num2    num1 >= num2
    """
    help """
    The operators go between two things: `+` [SUM], `-` [DIFFERENCE], `*` [PRODUCT] and `/` [QUOTIENT] for arithmetic, and `=` [EQUALP], `<>` [NOTEQUALP], `<` [LESSP], `>` [GREATERP], `<=` [LESSEQUALP] and `>=` [GREATEREQUALP] for comparing.

    `*` and `/` are worked out before `+` and `-`, which come before comparisons, so `2 + 3 * 4` is 14. Use [parentheses] to change that.

    A `-` with a space before it and none after, as in `FD -10`, is a minus sign, not a take-away: see [DIFFERENCE].
    """
    example title="Doing sums" """
    PRINT 2 + 3 * 4
    PRINT 10 / 4
    PRINT 3 * 3 = 9
    """
    see "parentheses" "minus"
  }
  comments {
    title "Comments: ;"
    eg "; a comment, to the end of the line"
    names ";"
    help """
    A semicolon starts a comment: everything after it on the line is for people to read, and Kame ignores it.
    """
    example title="Notes in a program" """
    ; A square, 150 steps along each side
    REPEAT 4 [FD 150 RT 90]   ; four turns of 90 make 360
    """
  }
  template-slots {
    title "Template slots: ? and ?REST"
    eg """
    ?  ?1  ?2  (? 3)
    ?REST  (?REST 2)
    """
    names "?" "?REST"
    help """
    In a template given to [MAP], [FOREACH], [FILTER] and the other template commands, `?` stands for the member being used. With several inputs, `?1` is the first, `?2` the second, and so on, or `(? 3)` for the third.

    `?REST` is the rest of the data after the member in `?`. `#`, or [REPCOUNT], is the member's position.
    """
    example title="Slots" """
    SHOW MAP [? * ?] [1 2 3 4]
    (FOREACH [a b c] [1 2 3] [PRINT WORD ?1 ?2])
    FOREACH [x y z] [SHOW ?REST]
    """
    see "map" "foreach" "apply"
  }
}

logo {
  data-structure-primitives {
    constructors {
      word {
        eg """
        WORD word1 word2
        (WORD word1 word2 word3 ...)
        """
        desc "outputs a word formed by concatenating its inputs."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs a word made by joining its inputs together, with no spaces between them. Give more than two inputs by putting the whole instruction in parentheses.

        Numbers are words too, so `WORD 1 2` is the word `12`.
        """
        example title="Joining words" """
        PRINT WORD "sun "flower
        PRINT (WORD "a "b "c "d)
        PRINT WORD "pen 2
        """
      }
      list {
        eg """
        LIST thing1 thing2
        (LIST thing1 thing2 thing3 ...)
        """
        desc "outputs a list whose members are its inputs, which can be any Logo datum (word, list, or array)."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs a list whose members are its inputs. Unlike [SENTENCE], a list input stays a list inside the new list.

        `[...]` makes a list too, but everything inside square brackets is taken as it's written, while `LIST` works out its inputs first.
        """
        example title="A list of things worked out" """
        MAKE "size 50
        SHOW LIST :size :size * 2
        SHOW (LIST "red [1 2] 3)
        """
        see "sentence" "fput" "lput"
      }
      sentence {
        eg """
        SENTENCE thing1 thing2
        SE thing1 thing2
        (SENTENCE thing1 thing2 thing3 ...)
        (SE thing1 thing2 thing3 ...)
        """
        desc "outputs a list whose members are its inputs, if those inputs are not lists, or the members of its inputs, if those inputs are lists."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs a list made by joining its inputs into one list. A word input becomes one member, and a list input gives all of its members, so lists are joined rather than nested.
        """
        example title="Building a sentence" """
        SHOW SENTENCE "hello [Kame the turtle]
        SHOW (SE [one two] [three] "four)
        """
        see "list" "word"
      }
      fput {
        eg "FPUT thing list"
        desc "outputs a list equal to its second input with one extra member, the first input, at the beginning. If the second input is a word, then the first input must be a one-letter word, and FPUT is equivalent to WORD."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the list with the thing put in front of its first member. The list itself isn't changed: you get a new one.
        """
        example title="Putting something first" """
        SHOW FPUT "a [b c d]
        """
        see "lput" "butfirst" "first"
      }
      lput {
        eg "LPUT thing list"
        desc "outputs a list equal to its second input with one extra member, the first input, at the end. If the second input is a word, then the first input must be a one-letter word, and LPUT is equivalent to WORD with its inputs in the other order."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the list with the thing added after its last member. The list itself isn't changed: you get a new one.
        """
        example title="Adding to the end" """
        MAKE "steps []
        REPEAT 4 [MAKE "steps LPUT REPCOUNT * 10 :steps]
        SHOW :steps
        """
        see "fput" "butlast" "last"
      }
      array {
        eg """
        ARRAY size
        (ARRAY size origin)
        """
        desc "outputs an array of size members (must be a positive integer), each of which initially is an empty list. Array members can be selected with ITEM and changed with SETITEM. The first member of the array is member number 1 unless an origin input (must be an integer) is given, in which case the first member of the array has that number as its index. (Typically 0 is used as the origin if anything.) Arrays are printed by PRINT and friends, and can be typed in, inside curly braces; indicate an origin with {a b c}@0."
        differs "Refuses to make more than 10 million members, so a typo such as ISEQ 1 1e12 is an error instead of running out of memory."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a new array with `size` members, each an empty list to start with. Change a member with [SETITEM], and get it with [ITEM].

        Arrays count from 1, unless you give an origin: `(ARRAY 5 0)` counts from 0. Arrays print in braces, `{...}`. Pendown won't make an array of more than 10 million members, so a typo can't use up all the memory.
        """
        example title="Filling an array" """
        MAKE "squares ARRAY 5
        REPEAT 5 [SETITEM REPCOUNT :squares REPCOUNT * REPCOUNT]
        SHOW :squares
        SHOW ITEM 3 :squares
        """
        see "listtoarray" "mdarray"
      }
      mdarray {
        eg """
        MDARRAY sizelist  (library procedure)
        (MDARRAY sizelist origin)
        """
        desc """
        outputs a multi-dimensional array. The first input must be a list of one or more positive integers. The second input, if present, must be a single integer that applies to every dimension of the array.

        Ex: (MDARRAY [3 5] 0) outputs a two-dimensional array whose members range from [0 0] to [2 4].
        """
        differs "Refuses to make more than 10 million members, so a typo such as ISEQ 1 1e12 is an error instead of running out of memory."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs an array of arrays: a grid. The first input lists how big each dimension is, so `MDARRAY [3 4]` is 3 rows of 4. Get and change members with [MDITEM] and [MDSETITEM], giving a list of indexes.

        Like [ARRAY], it counts from 1 unless you give an origin, which is used for every dimension.
        """
        example title="A small grid" """
        MAKE "grid MDARRAY [2 3]
        MDSETITEM [1 2] :grid "x
        MDSETITEM [2 3] :grid "o
        SHOW :grid
        """
      }
      listtoarray {
        eg """
        LISTTOARRAY list
        (LISTTOARRAY list origin)
        """
        desc "outputs an array of the same size as the input list, whose members are the members of the input list."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs an array with the same members as the list. Give an origin as a second input, in parentheses, to count from something other than 1.
        """
        example title="From a list to an array" """
        MAKE "colours LISTTOARRAY [red green blue]
        SETITEM 2 :colours "yellow
        SHOW :colours
        """
        see "arraytolist" "array"
      }
      arraytolist {
        eg "ARRAYTOLIST array"
        desc "outputs a list whose members are the members of the input array. The first member of the output is the first member of the array, regardless of the array’s origin."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a list with the same members as the array, in order, whatever the array's origin.
        """
        example title="From an array to a list" """
        SHOW ARRAYTOLIST {10 20 30}
        """
        see "listtoarray"
      }
      combine {
        eg "COMBINE thing1 thing2  (library procedure)"
        desc "if thing2 is a word, outputs WORD thing1 thing2. If thing2 is a list, outputs FPUT thing1 thing2."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Joins a thing onto the front of a word or list: if the second input is a word, it's [WORD]; if it's a list, it's [FPUT].
        """
        example title="Words and lists" """
        SHOW COMBINE "sun "flower
        SHOW COMBINE "sun [flower]
        """
      }
      reverse {
        eg "REVERSE list  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs a list whose members are the members of the input list, in reverse order."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the list with its members in the opposite order. A word comes out backwards too.
        """
        example title="Backwards" """
        SHOW REVERSE [1 2 3 4]
        PRINT REVERSE "turtle
        """
      }
      gensym {
        eg "GENSYM  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs a unique word each time it’s invoked. The words are of the form G1, G2, etc."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    data-selectors {
      first {
        eg "FIRST thing"
        desc "if the input is a word, outputs the first character of the word. If the input is a list, outputs the first member of the list. If the input is an array, outputs the origin of the array (that is, the index of the first member of the array)."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the first member of a list, or the first character of a word.

        For an array, it outputs the array's origin, the number its first member has, which is usually 1.
        """
        example title="The first one" """
        PRINT FIRST [apple banana cherry]
        PRINT FIRST "kame
        """
        see "last" "butfirst" "item"
      }
      firsts {
        eg "FIRSTS list"
        desc """
        outputs a list containing the FIRST of each member of the input list. It is an error if any member of the input list is empty. (The input itself may be empty, in which case the output is also empty.) This could be written as

            to firsts :list
            output map "first :list
            end

        but is provided as a primitive in order to speed up the iteration tools MAP, MAP.SE, and FOREACH.

            to transpose :matrix
            if emptyp first :matrix [op []]
            op fput firsts :matrix transpose bfs :matrix
            end
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a list of the first member of each member of the input. Every member must have a first: none can be empty.
        """
        example title="Firsts of each" """
        SHOW FIRSTS [[1 2 3] [4 5 6] [7 8 9]]
        SHOW FIRSTS [apple banana cherry]
        """
        see "butfirsts" "first"
      }
      last {
        eg "LAST wordorlist"
        desc "if the input is a word, outputs the last character of the word. If the input is a list, outputs the last member of the list."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the last member of a list, or the last character of a word.
        """
        example title="The last one" """
        PRINT LAST [apple banana cherry]
        PRINT LAST "kame
        """
        see "first" "butlast"
      }
      butfirst {
        eg """
        BUTFIRST wordorlist
        BF wordorlist
        """
        desc "if the input is a word, outputs a word containing all but the first character of the input. If the input is a list, outputs a list containing all but the first member of the input."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs everything except the first member of a list, or the first character of a word.

        It's how a procedure works through a list one member at a time: use [FIRST], then call itself with the `BUTFIRST`.
        """
        example title="All but the first" """
        SHOW BUTFIRST [apple banana cherry]
        PRINT BF "kame
        """
        example title="Working through a list" """
        TO SAY.EACH :things
          IF EMPTYP :things [STOP]
          PRINT FIRST :things
          SAY.EACH BUTFIRST :things
        END
        SAY.EACH [red green blue]
        """
        see "first" "butlast" "emptyp"
      }
      butfirsts {
        eg """
        BUTFIRSTS list
        BFS list
        """
        desc """
        outputs a list containing the BUTFIRST of each member of the input list. It is an error if any member of the input list is empty or an array. (The input itself may be empty, in which case the output is also empty.) This could be written as

            to butfirsts :list
            output map "butfirst :list
            end

        but is provided as a primitive in order to speed up the iteration tools MAP, MAP.SE, and FOREACH.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a list of the [BUTFIRST] of each member of the input. Every member must have a first to drop: none can be empty.
        """
        example title="All but the first of each" """
        SHOW BUTFIRSTS [[1 2 3] [4 5 6]]
        """
        see "firsts" "butfirst"
      }
      butlast {
        eg """
        BUTLAST wordorlist
        BL wordorlist
        """
        desc "if the input is a word, outputs a word containing all but the last character of the input. If the input is a list, outputs a list containing all but the last member of the input."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs everything except the last member of a list, or the last character of a word.
        """
        example title="All but the last" """
        SHOW BUTLAST [apple banana cherry]
        PRINT BL "kamel
        """
        see "last" "butfirst"
      }
      item {
        eg "ITEM index thing"
        desc "if the thing is a word, outputs the indexth character of the word. If the thing is a list, outputs the indexth member of the list. If the thing is an array, outputs the indexth member of the array. Index starts at 1 for words and lists; the starting index of an array is specified when the array is created."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the member at a position in a list or array, or the character at a position in a word. Lists and words count from 1. An array counts from its origin, which is 1 unless it was made with another.
        """
        example title="Picking by position" """
        PRINT ITEM 2 [apple banana cherry]
        PRINT ITEM 3 "turtle
        """
        example title="A colour for each side" """
        MAKE "colours [red orange yellow green blue]
        REPEAT 5 [
          SETPC ITEM REPCOUNT :colours
          FD 150 RT 72
        ]
        """
        see "first" "last" "setitem"
      }
      mditem {
        eg "MDITEM indexlist array  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs the member of the multidimensional array selected by the list of numbers indexlist."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a member of a multi-dimensional array, chosen by a list of indexes, one for each dimension.
        """
        example title="A member of a grid" """
        MAKE "grid MDARRAY [3 3]
        MDSETITEM [2 2] :grid "middle
        PRINT MDITEM [2 2] :grid
        """
        see "mdarray" "mdsetitem"
      }
      pick {
        eg "PICK list  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs a randomly chosen member of the input list."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a member of the list chosen at random. Each member has the same chance.
        """
        example title="A random colour each time" """
        REPEAT 36 [
          SETPC PICK [red orange yellow green blue purple]
          FD 200 BK 200 RT 10
        ]
        """
        see "random"
      }
      remove {
        eg "REMOVE thing list  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs a copy of list with every member equal to thing removed."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the list without any members equal to the thing. For a word, it removes those characters.
        """
        example title="Taking things out" """
        SHOW REMOVE "b [a b c b a]
        PRINT REMOVE "e "elephant
        """
        see "remdup"
      }
      remdup {
        eg "REMDUP list  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs a copy of list with duplicate members removed. If two or more members of the input are equal, the rightmost of those members is the one that remains in the output."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the list with duplicates removed. Where a member appears more than once, the last one is the one kept.
        """
        example title="Each once" """
        SHOW REMDUP [a b a c b d]
        """
        see "remove"
      }
      quoted {
        eg "QUOTED thing  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs its input, if a list; outputs its input with a quotation mark prepended, if a word."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a word with a quotation mark in front of it, or a list unchanged. It's useful when you build instructions to [RUN].
        """
        example title="Adding a quote" """
        SHOW QUOTED "kame
        SHOW QUOTED [a b]
        """
      }
    }
    data-mutators {
      setitem {
        eg "SETITEM index array value"
        desc "command. Replaces the indexth member of array with the new value. Ensures that the resulting array is not circular, i.e., value may not be a list or array that contains array."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Changes the member at a position in an array to a new value. The array changes in place, so every variable holding that array sees the change.

        Only arrays can be changed like this. You can't put an array inside itself.
        """
        example title="Changing a member" """
        MAKE "row {a b c}
        SETITEM 2 :row "z
        SHOW :row
        """
        see "item" "array" "mdsetitem"
      }
      mdsetitem {
        eg "MDSETITEM indexlist array value  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Replaces the member of array chosen by indexlist with the new value."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Changes a member of a multi-dimensional array, chosen by a list of indexes, one for each dimension.
        """
        example title="Marking a grid" """
        MAKE "board MDARRAY [3 3]
        MDSETITEM [1 1] :board "x
        MDSETITEM [2 2] :board "o
        SHOW :board
        """
        see "mdarray" "mditem"
      }
      .setfirst {
        eg ".SETFIRST list value"
        desc """
        command. Changes the first member of list to be value.

        WARNING: Primitives whose names start with a period are dangerous. Their use by non-experts is not recommended. The use of .SETFIRST can lead to circular list structures, which will get some Logo primitives into infinite loops, and to unexpected changes to other data structures that share storage with the list being modified.
        """
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it changes a list in place, which can make a list contain itself. Use an array and SETITEM instead."
      }
      .setbf {
        eg ".SETBF list value"
        desc """
        command. Changes the butfirst of list to be value.

        WARNING: Primitives whose names start with a period are dangerous. Their use by non-experts is not recommended. The use of .SETBF can lead to circular list structures, which will get some Logo primitives into infinite loops; unexpected changes to other data structures that share storage with the list being modified; or to Logo crashes and coredumps if the butfirst of a list is not itself a list.
        """
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it changes a list in place, which can make a list contain itself. Use an array and SETITEM instead."
      }
      .setitem {
        eg ".SETITEM index array value"
        desc """
        command. Changes the indexth member of array to be value, like SETITEM, but without checking for circularity.

        WARNING: Primitives whose names start with a period are dangerous. Their use by non-experts is not recommended. The use of .SETITEM can lead to circular arrays, which will get some Logo primitives into infinite loops.
        """
        see "setitem"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it's a faster SETITEM that doesn't check its inputs. Use SETITEM."
      }
      push {
        eg "PUSH stackname thing  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Adds the thing to the stack that is the value of the variable whose name is stackname. This variable must have a list as its value; the initial value should be the empty list. New members are added at the front of the list."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Adds a thing to the front of a list kept in a variable, as a stack. The first input is the variable's name, quoted. Start the variable as an empty list.

        [POP] takes things back off, most recent first.
        """
        example title="A stack of plates" """
        MAKE "plates []
        PUSH "plates "blue
        PUSH "plates "red
        SHOW :plates
        PRINT POP "plates
        SHOW :plates
        """
        see "pop" "queue"
      }
      pop {
        eg "POP stackname  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs the most recently PUSHed member of the stack that is the value of the variable whose name is stackname and removes that member from the stack."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the thing most recently added to a stack with [PUSH], and removes it. The input is the variable's name, quoted.
        """
        example title="Last in, first out" """
        MAKE "stack []
        PUSH "stack 1
        PUSH "stack 2
        PUSH "stack 3
        PRINT POP "stack
        PRINT POP "stack
        """
        see "push" "dequeue"
      }
      queue {
        eg "QUEUE queuename thing  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Adds the thing to the queue that is the value of the variable whose name is queuename. This variable must have a list as its value; the initial value should be the empty list. New members are added at the back of the list."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Adds a thing to the end of a list kept in a variable, as a queue. The first input is the variable's name, quoted. Start the variable as an empty list.

        [DEQUEUE] takes things back off, oldest first.
        """
        example title="Waiting in line" """
        MAKE "line []
        QUEUE "line "ann
        QUEUE "line "bob
        QUEUE "line "cy
        PRINT DEQUEUE "line
        SHOW :line
        """
        see "dequeue" "push"
      }
      dequeue {
        eg "DEQUEUE queuename  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs the least recently QUEUEd member of the queue that is the value of the variable whose name is queuename and removes that member from the queue."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the thing added to a queue longest ago with [QUEUE], and removes it. The input is the variable's name, quoted.
        """
        example title="First in, first out" """
        MAKE "jobs []
        QUEUE "jobs "draw
        QUEUE "jobs "colour
        PRINT DEQUEUE "jobs
        PRINT DEQUEUE "jobs
        """
        see "queue" "pop"
      }
    }
    predicates {
      wordp {
        eg """
        WORDP thing
        WORD? thing
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is a word, FALSE otherwise."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the input is a word, and `FALSE` if it isn't. Numbers are words.
        """
        example title="Is it a word?" """
        PRINT WORDP "kame
        PRINT WORDP 42
        PRINT WORDP [kame]
        """
        see "listp" "numberp"
      }
      listp {
        eg """
        LISTP thing
        LIST? thing
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is a list, FALSE otherwise."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the input is a list, and `FALSE` if it isn't.
        """
        example title="Is it a list?" """
        PRINT LISTP [1 2 3]
        PRINT LISTP "kame
        """
        see "wordp" "arrayp"
      }
      arrayp {
        eg """
        ARRAYP thing
        ARRAY? thing
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is an array, FALSE otherwise."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the input is an array, and `FALSE` if it isn't.
        """
        example title="Is it an array?" """
        PRINT ARRAYP {1 2 3}
        PRINT ARRAYP [1 2 3]
        """
        see "listp"
      }
      emptyp {
        eg """
        EMPTYP thing
        EMPTY? thing
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is the empty word or the empty list, FALSE otherwise."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the input is the empty word or the empty list, and `FALSE` otherwise.

        It's how a procedure that works through a list knows when to stop.
        """
        example title="Is it empty?" """
        PRINT EMPTYP []
        PRINT EMPTYP "
        PRINT EMPTYP [kame]
        """
        see "butfirst" "count"
      }
      equalp {
        eg """
        EQUALP thing1 thing2
        EQUAL? thing1 thing2
        thing1 = thing2
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the inputs are equal, FALSE otherwise. Two numbers are equal if they have the same numeric value. Two non-numeric words are equal if they contain the same characters in the same order. If there is a variable named CASEIGNOREDP whose value is TRUE, then an upper case letter is considered the same as the corresponding lower case letter. (This is the case by default.) Two lists are equal if their members are equal. An array is only equal to itself; two separately created arrays are never equal even if their members are equal. (It is important to be able to know if two expressions have the same array as their value because arrays are mutable; if, for example, two variables have the same array as their values then performing SETITEM on one of them will also change the other.)"
        differs "Numbers are compared rounded to 15 significant digits, so two numbers are equal exactly when they print the same: 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3 is TRUE. In UCBLogo it is FALSE, because the two differ in the 17th digit."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if its inputs are equal, and `FALSE` if they aren't. `=` between two things does the same.

        Numbers are equal if they have the same value, so `2 = 2.0` is `TRUE`. They're compared to 15 significant digits, so two numbers are equal exactly when they print the same: `0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3` is `TRUE`. Words are equal if they have the same characters, ignoring upper and lower case while [CASEIGNOREDP] is `TRUE`, as it is to start with. Lists are equal if their members are. An array is only equal to itself.
        """
        example title="What's equal" """
        PRINT 2 = 2.0
        PRINT "kame = "KAME
        PRINT [1 2] = [1 2]
        PRINT 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3
        """
        see "notequalp"
      }
      notequalp {
        eg """
        NOTEQUALP thing1 thing2
        NOTEQUAL? thing1 thing2
        thing1 <> thing2
        """
        desc "outputs FALSE if the inputs are equal, TRUE otherwise. See EQUALP for the meaning of equality for different data types."
        differs "Numbers are compared rounded to 15 significant digits, as EQUALP does, so this always agrees with =."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if its inputs aren't equal, and `FALSE` if they are. `<>` between two things does the same. See [EQUALP] for what equal means.
        """
        example title="Not equal" """
        PRINT 3 <> 4
        PRINT "a <> "A
        """
        see "equalp"
      }
      beforep {
        eg """
        BEFOREP word1 word2
        BEFORE? word1 word2
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if word1 comes before word2 in ASCII collating sequence (for words of letters, in alphabetical order). Case-sensitivity is determined by the value of CASEIGNOREDP. Note that if the inputs are numbers, the result may not be the same as with LESSP; for example, BEFOREP 3 12 is false because 3 collates after 1."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the first word comes before the second in alphabetical order. Upper and lower case count as the same while [CASEIGNOREDP] is `TRUE`.

        Numbers are compared as words, character by character, so `BEFOREP 3 12` is `FALSE`. Use `<` to compare numbers.
        """
        example title="Alphabetical order" """
        PRINT BEFOREP "apple "banana
        PRINT BEFOREP "zebra "ant
        PRINT BEFOREP 3 12
        """
      }
      .eq {
        eg ".EQ thing1 thing2"
        desc """
        outputs TRUE if its two inputs are the same datum, so that applying a mutator to one will change the other as well. Outputs FALSE otherwise, even if the inputs are equal in value.

        WARNING: Primitives whose names start with a period are dangerous. Their use by non-experts is not recommended. The use of mutators can lead to circular data structures, infinite loops, or Logo crashes.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      memberp {
        eg """
        MEMBERP thing1 thing2
        MEMBER? thing1 thing2
        """
        desc "if thing2 is a list or an array, outputs TRUE if thing1 is EQUALP to a member of thing2, FALSE otherwise. If thing2 is a word, outputs TRUE if thing1 is a one-character word EQUALP to a character of thing2, FALSE otherwise."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the first input is a member of the second, a list or array, and `FALSE` if it isn't. If the second input is a word, it checks for a one-character word in it.
        """
        example title="Is it in there?" """
        PRINT MEMBERP "blue [red green blue]
        PRINT MEMBERP "e "kame
        PRINT MEMBERP "z "kame
        """
        see "member" "substringp"
      }
      substringp {
        eg """
        SUBSTRINGP thing1 thing2
        SUBSTRING? thing1 thing2
        """
        desc "if thing1 or thing2 is a list or an array, outputs FALSE. If thing2 is a word, outputs TRUE if thing1 is EQUALP to a substring of thing2, FALSE otherwise."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the first word appears inside the second, and `FALSE` if it doesn't, or if either is a list.
        """
        example title="Part of a word" """
        PRINT SUBSTRINGP "own "pendown
        PRINT SUBSTRINGP "up "pendown
        """
        see "memberp"
      }
      numberp {
        eg """
        NUMBERP thing
        NUMBER? thing
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is a number, FALSE otherwise."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the input is a number, and `FALSE` if it isn't.
        """
        example title="Is it a number?" """
        PRINT NUMBERP 42
        PRINT NUMBERP "-3.5
        PRINT NUMBERP "kame
        """
        see "wordp"
      }
      vbarredp {
        eg """
        VBARREDP char
        VBARRED? char
        BACKSLASHEDP char  (library procedure)
        BACKSLASHED? char  (library procedure)
        """
        desc """
        outputs TRUE if the input character was originally entered into Logo within vertical bars (|) to prevent its usual special syntactic meaning, FALSE otherwise. (Outputs TRUE only if the character is a backslashed space, tab, newline, or one of ()[]+-*/=<>":;\\~?| )

        The names BACKSLASHEDP and BACKSLASHED? are included in the Logo library for backward compatibility with the former names of this primitive, although it does not output TRUE for characters originally entered with backslashes.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    queries {
      count {
        eg "COUNT thing"
        desc "outputs the number of characters in the input, if the input is a word; outputs the number of members in the input, if it is a list or an array. (For an array, this may or may not be the index of the last member, depending on the array’s origin.)"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs how many members a list or array has, or how many characters a word has.
        """
        example title="How many?" """
        PRINT COUNT [red green blue]
        PRINT COUNT "turtle
        PRINT COUNT []
        """
        see "item" "emptyp"
      }
      ascii {
        eg "ASCII char"
        desc "outputs the integer (between 0 and 255) that represents the input character in the ASCII code. Interprets control characters as representing vbarred punctuation, and returns the character code for the corresponding punctuation character without vertical bars. (Compare RAWASCII.)"
        differs "Outputs the character's Unicode code point, which is its ASCII code for ASCII characters."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the number that stands for a character: its Unicode code point, which for ordinary letters and digits is its ASCII code.
        """
        example title="Character codes" """
        PRINT ASCII "A
        PRINT ASCII "a
        """
        see "char"
      }
      rawascii {
        eg "RAWASCII char"
        desc "outputs the integer (between 0 and 255) that represents the input character in the ASCII code. Interprets control characters as representing themselves. To find out the ASCII code of an arbitrary keystroke, use RAWASCII RC."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      char {
        eg "CHAR int"
        desc "outputs the character represented in the ASCII code by the input, which must be an integer between 0 and 255."
        differs "Takes any Unicode code point, not just ASCII codes."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the character a number stands for: any Unicode code point, not just ASCII codes.
        """
        example title="From codes to characters" """
        PRINT CHAR 75
        REPEAT 5 [TYPE CHAR 64 + REPCOUNT]
        PRINT "
        """
        see "ascii"
      }
      member {
        eg "MEMBER thing1 thing2"
        desc "if thing2 is a word or list and if MEMBERP with these inputs would output TRUE, outputs the portion of thing2 from the first instance of thing1 to the end. If MEMBERP would output FALSE, outputs the empty word or list according to the type of thing2. It is an error for thing2 to be an array."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Looks for the first input in the second, a word or list, and outputs the rest of it from there on. If it isn't there, it outputs an empty word or list.
        """
        example title="From there on" """
        SHOW MEMBER "c [a b c d e]
        PRINT MEMBER "d "pendown
        """
        see "memberp"
      }
      lowercase {
        eg "LOWERCASE word"
        desc "outputs a copy of the input word, but with all uppercase letters changed to the corresponding lowercase letter."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the word with every upper case letter made lower case.
        """
        example title="Quietly" """
        PRINT LOWERCASE "KAME
        """
        see "uppercase"
      }
      uppercase {
        eg "UPPERCASE word"
        desc "outputs a copy of the input word, but with all lowercase letters changed to the corresponding uppercase letter."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the word with every lower case letter made upper case.
        """
        example title="Loudly" """
        PRINT UPPERCASE "kame
        """
        see "lowercase"
      }
      standout {
        eg "STANDOUT thing"
        desc """
        outputs a word that, when printed, will appear like the input but displayed in standout mode (boldface, reverse video, or whatever your version does for standout). The word contains machine-specific magic characters at the beginning and end; in between is the printed form (as if displayed using TYPE) of the input. The output is always a word, even if the input is of some other type, but it may include spaces and other formatting characters. Note: a word output by STANDOUT while Logo is running on one machine will probably not have the desired effect if printed on another type of machine.

        In the Macintosh classic version, the way that standout works is incompatible with the use of characters whose ASCII code is greater than 127. Therefore, you have a choice to make: The instruction

            CANINVERSE 0

        disables standout, but enables the display of ASCII codes above 127, and the instruction

            CANINVERSE 1

        restores the default situation in which standout is enabled and the extra graphic characters cannot be printed.
        """
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it makes bold or inverse text in a terminal, and Pendown's console isn't a terminal."
      }
      parse {
        eg "PARSE word"
        desc "outputs the list that would result if the input word were entered in response to a READLIST operation. That is, PARSE READWORD has the same value as READLIST for the same characters read."
        see "readlist"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      runparse {
        eg "RUNPARSE wordorlist"
        desc "outputs the list that would result if the input word or list were entered as an instruction line; characters such as infix operators and parentheses are separate members of the output. Note that sublists of a runparsed list are not themselves runparsed."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
  }
  objects {
    constructors {
      kindof {
        eg """
        KINDOF object
        KINDOF objectlist
        (KINDOF object1 object2 ...)
        """
        desc "creates and outputs an object whose parent is object, or whose parents are object1, object2, etc., or the elements of objectlist. There must be at least one input, and if a list, it may not be empty."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      something {
        eg "SOMETHING"
        desc "creates and outputs an object whose parent is the Logo object. make \"foo something is equivalent to make \"foo kindof logo."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      oneof {
        eg """
        ONEOF object
        ONEOF objectlist
        (ONEOF object input1 input2 ...)
        (ONEOF objectlist input1 input2 ...)
        """
        desc """
        creates an object whose parent is object or whose parents are the elements of objectlist. Asks the new object to exist, and then outputs the object. Before the object is Asked to exist, all remaining inputs after the first are collected into a list, which is made the value of the global variable initlist. The convention is that initlist contains alternating names and values, which serve to initialize the newly created object.

        The output of oneof is usually called an instance.
        """
        see "exist"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    mutators {
      exist {
        eg "exist"
        desc """
        Initializes a new object. It is run automatically by oneof.

        The default exist procedure creates object-local variables with the odd-numbered elements of initlist as names, and the corresponding even-numbered elements as values. Thus

            make "obj (oneof logo "foo "bar "baz "garply)

        will create a new object and then tell it to exist, which will in effect tell it to

            havemake "foo "bar
            havemake "baz "garply

        Example: Objects do not usually keep track of their instances. To make one that does, you can specialize exist:

            ? make "recordingobject something
            ? ask :recordingobject [havemake "instances []]
            ? ask :recordingobject [to exist]
            > usual.exist
            > make "instances [fput self :instances]
            > end
        """
        see "oneof" "usual" "havemake"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      have {
        eg """
        HAVE name
        HAVE namelist
        """
        desc "tells the current object to create an object variable named name, or object variables whose names are the elements of namelist."
        see "havemake"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      havemake {
        eg "HAVEMAKE name value"
        desc """
        tells the current object to create an object variable named name, and give it the value value. Since procedure input values are computed before the procedure is run, the value expression may refer to the object’s parent’s variable named name:

            havemake "size :size/2
        """
        see "have" "make"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    selectors {
      self {
        eg "SELF"
        desc "outputs the current object (not its name!)."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      parents {
        eg "PARENTS"
        desc """
        outputs a list containing the parent(s) of the current object. All objects except for logo have at least one parent. Note that the elements of the output list are objects, not names of objects.

        Examples:

            to grandparents
            output remdup (map.se [ask ? [parents]] parents)
            end

            to ancestors
            if emptyp parents [output (list self)]   ; can happen only if self=logo
            output remdup sentence parents map.se [ask ? [ancestors]] parents
            end
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      mynames {
        eg "MYNAMES"
        desc "output a list of the names of the object variables owned (not inherited) by the current object."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      mynamep {
        eg """
        MYNAMEP name
        MYNAME? name
        """
        desc "outputs true if name is the name of an object variable owned (not inherited) by the current object, false otherwise."
        see "mynames"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      myprocs {
        eg "MYPROCS"
        desc "outputs a list of the names of the procedures (methods) owned by (not inherited by) the current object."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      myprocp {
        eg """
        MYPROCP name
        MYPROC? name
        """
        desc "outputs true if name is the name of a procedure (a method) owned (not inherited) by the current object, false otherwise."
        see "myprocs"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    messages {
      talkto {
        eg "TALKTO object"
        desc "changes the current object to object. (Note that the input is an object, not the name of an object.) Talkto can be used only at toplevel or within a pause (when typing into a Logo prompt, not inside a procedure)."
        see "ask"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      ask {
        eg "ASK object runlist"
        desc "command or operation. Temporarily sets the current object to object while running the instructions or expression in runlist. If runlist is an expression, then ask outputs its value. As soon as runlist finishes, the current object is set back to its previous value."
        see "talkto"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    queries {
      whosename {
        eg "WHOSENAME name"
        desc "outputs the object that owns the currently accessible variable named name. If there is no such accessible variable, or it’s a procedure-local variable, an error is signalled."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      whoseproc {
        eg "WHOSEPROC name"
        desc "outputs the object that owns the currently accessible procedure named name. If there is no such procedure, an error is signalled."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    usual {
      eg """
      to foo ...
      ...
      USUAL.foo ...
      ...
      end
      """
      desc """
      Usual is not a procedure. It’s a special notation that can be used only inside a procedure definition; the word usual must be followed by a period and then the name of the procedure being defined. (That is, usual.foo can be used only inside the definition of foo.) It refers to the procedure that would be inherited from a parent if this (re)definition didn’t exist; it allows a specialized method to invoke the ordinary version.

          make "bigturtle kindof turtle
          ask :bigturtle [to forward :length]
          usual.forward 2 * :length
          end

      If there is no inherited procedure of the same name, then calling usual.___ does nothing if used as a command, or outputs an empty list if used as an operation.

      If an object has multiple parents, the behavior of usual may be confusing. Suppose the current object has two parents, A and B, in that order. Then usual.foo in the current object’s own foo method refers to object A’s foo, but usual.foo within object A’s foo refers to object B’s foo in this situation, even though A’s parent isn’t B.

          make "dashedturtle kindof turtle
          ask :dashedturtle [to forward :length]
          if not pendownp [usual.forward :length stop]
          if :length <= 5 [usual.forward :length stop]
          usual.forward 5
          penup
          usual.forward (ifelse :length <= 10 [:length-5] [5])
          pendown
          if :length > 10 [forward :length-10]    ; Note no USUAL here.
          end

          ask :dashedturtle [forward 25]
          - - -âº

          make "bigdashed oneof (list :bigturtle :dashedturtle)
          make "dashedbig oneof (list :dashedturtle :bigturtle)
          ask :bigdashed [forward 25]
          - - - - - âº
          ask :dashedbig [forward 25]
          --  --  --âº
      """
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 7
    }
  }
  communication {
    transmitters {
      print {
        eg """
        PRINT thing
        PR thing
        (PRINT thing1 thing2 ...)
        (PR thing1 thing2 ...)
        """
        desc "command. Prints the input or inputs to the current write stream (initially the screen). All the inputs are printed on a single line, separated by spaces, ending with a newline. If an input is a list, square brackets are not printed around it, but brackets are printed around sublists. Braces are always printed around arrays."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Prints its input in the console, then starts a new line. A list is printed without its outer brackets, so `PRINT [Hello Kame]` prints `Hello Kame`.

        Put the instruction in parentheses to print several things on one line, with spaces between them.
        """
        example title="Hello" """
        PRINT [Hello, Kame!]
        PRINT 6 * 7
        (PRINT "Kame "is 100 "steps "tall)
        """
        see "show" "type"
      }
      type {
        eg """
        TYPE thing
        (TYPE thing1 thing2 ...)
        """
        desc "command. Prints the input or inputs like PRINT, except that no newline character is printed at the end and multiple inputs are not separated by spaces. Note: printing to the screen is ordinarily line buffered; that is, the characters you print using TYPE will not actually appear on the screen until either a newline character is printed (for example, by PRINT or SHOW) or Logo tries to read from the keyboard (either at the request of your program or after an instruction prompt). This buffering makes the program much faster than it would be if each character appeared immediately, and in most cases the effect is not disconcerting. To accommodate programs that do a lot of positioned text display using TYPE, Logo will force printing whenever SETCURSOR is invoked. This solves most buffering problems. Still, on occasion you may find it necessary to force the buffered characters to be printed explicitly; this can be done using the WAIT command. WAIT 0 will force printing without actually waiting."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Prints its input in the console like [PRINT], but doesn't start a new line afterwards, so the next thing printed carries on the same line. With several inputs, it puts no spaces between them.
        """
        example title="All on one line" """
        REPEAT 5 [TYPE REPCOUNT]
        PRINT "...
        """
        see "print"
      }
      show {
        eg """
        SHOW thing
        (SHOW thing1 thing2 ...)
        """
        desc "command. Prints the input or inputs like PRINT, except that if an input is a list it is printed inside square brackets."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Prints its input in the console like [PRINT], but shows a list with its brackets, so you can see that it's a list.
        """
        example title="Seeing the brackets" """
        SHOW [1 2 3]
        PRINT [1 2 3]
        SHOW POS
        """
        see "print"
      }
    }
    receivers {
      readlist {
        eg """
        READLIST
        RL
        """
        desc "reads a line from the read stream (initially the keyboard) and outputs that line as a list. The line is separated into members as though it were typed in square brackets in an instruction. If the read stream is a file, and the end of file is reached, READLIST outputs the empty word (not the empty list). READLIST processes backslash, vertical bar, and tilde characters in the read stream; the output list will not contain these characters but they will have had their usual effect. READLIST does not, however, treat semicolon as a comment character."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      readword {
        eg """
        READWORD
        RW
        """
        desc "reads a line from the read stream and outputs that line as a word. The output is a single word even if the line contains spaces, brackets, etc. If the read stream is a file, and the end of file is reached, READWORD outputs the empty list (not the empty word). READWORD processes backslash, vertical bar, and tilde characters in the read stream. In the case of a tilde used for line continuation, the output word does include the tilde and the newline characters, so that the user program can tell exactly what the user entered. Vertical bars in the line are also preserved in the output. Backslash characters are not preserved in the output."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      readrawline {
        eg "READRAWLINE"
        desc "reads a line from the read stream and outputs that line as a word. The output is a single word even if the line contains spaces, brackets, etc. If the read stream is a file, and the end of file is reached, READRAWLINE outputs the empty list (not the empty word). READRAWLINE outputs the exact string of characters as they appear in the line, with no special meaning for backslash, vertical bar, tilde, or any other formatting characters."
        see "readword"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      readchar {
        eg """
        READCHAR
        RC
        """
        desc "reads a single character from the read stream and outputs that character as a word. If the read stream is a file, and the end of file is reached, READCHAR outputs the empty list (not the empty word). If the read stream is the keyboard, echoing is turned off when READCHAR is invoked, and remains off until READLIST or READWORD is invoked or a Logo prompt is printed. Backslash, vertical bar, and tilde characters have no special meaning in this context."
        see "readlist"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      readchars {
        eg """
        READCHARS num
        RCS num
        """
        desc "reads num characters from the read stream and outputs those characters as a word. If the read stream is a file, and the end of file is reached, READCHARS outputs the empty list (not the empty word). If the read stream is the keyboard, echoing is turned off when READCHARS is invoked, and remains off until READLIST or READWORD is invoked or a Logo prompt is printed. Backslash, vertical bar, and tilde characters have no special meaning in this context."
        see "readlist" "readword"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      shell {
        eg """
        SHELL command
        (SHELL command wordflag)
        """
        desc """
        Under Unix, outputs the result of running command as a shell command. (The command is sent to ‘/bin/sh’, not ‘csh’ or other alternatives.) If the command is a literal list in the instruction line, and if you want a backslash character sent to the shell, you must use \\\\ to get the backslash through Logo’s reader intact. The output is a list containing one member for each line generated by the shell command. Ordinarily each such line is represented by a list in the output, as though the line were read using READLIST. If a second input is given, regardless of the value of the input, each line is represented by a word in the output as though it were read with READWORD. Example:

            to dayofweek
            output first first shell [date]
            end

        This is first first to extract the first word of the first (and only) line of the shell output.

        Under MacOS X, SHELL works as under Unix. SHELL is not available under Mac Classic.

        Under DOS, SHELL is a command, not an operation; it sends its input to a DOS command processor but does not collect the result of the command.

        Under Windows, the wxWidgets version of Logo behaves as under Unix (except that DOS-style commands are understood; use dir rather than ls). The non-wxWidgets version behaves like the DOS version.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    file-access {
      setprefix {
        eg "SETPREFIX string"
        desc "command. Sets a prefix that will be used as the implicit beginning of filenames in OPENREAD, OPENWRITE, OPENAPPEND, OPENUPDATE, LOAD, and SAVE commands. Logo will put the appropriate separator character (slash for Unix, backslash for DOS/Windows, colon for MacOS Classic) between the prefix and the filename entered by the user. The input to SETPREFIX must be a word, unless it is the empty list, to indicate that there should be no prefix."
        see "openread" "openwrite" "openappend" "openupdate" "load" "save"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it sets a folder for file names, for old file systems. File names will be relative to your program's file."
      }
      prefix {
        eg "PREFIX"
        desc "outputs the current file prefix, or [] if there is no prefix."
        see "setprefix"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it sets a folder for file names, for old file systems. File names will be relative to your program's file."
      }
      openread {
        eg "OPENREAD filename"
        desc "command. Opens the named file for reading. The read position is initially at the beginning of the file."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      openwrite {
        eg "OPENWRITE filename"
        desc """
        command. Opens the named file for writing. If the file already existed, the old version is deleted and a new, empty file created.

        OPENWRITE, but not the other OPEN variants, will accept as input a two-element list, in which the first element must be a variable name, and the second must be a positive integer. A character buffer of the specified size will be created. When a SETWRITE is done with this same list (in the sense of .EQ, not a copy, so you must do something like

            ? make "buf [foo 100]
            ? openwrite :buf
            ? setwrite :buf
                [...]
            ? close :buf

        and not just

            ? openwrite [foo 100]
            ? setwrite [foo 100]

        and so on), the printed characters are stored in the buffer; when a CLOSE is done with the same list as input, the characters from the buffer (treated as one long word, even if spaces and newlines are included) become the value of the specified variable.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      openappend {
        eg "OPENAPPEND filename"
        desc "command. Opens the named file for writing. If the file already exists, the write position is initially set to the end of the old file, so that newly written data will be appended to it."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      openupdate {
        eg "OPENUPDATE filename"
        desc "command. Opens the named file for reading and writing. The read and write position is initially set to the end of the old file, if any. Note: each open file has only one position, for both reading and writing. If a file opened for update is both READER and WRITER at the same time, then SETREADPOS will also affect WRITEPOS and vice versa. Also, if you alternate reading and writing the same file, you must SETREADPOS between a write and a read, and SETWRITEPOS between a read and a write."
        see "reader" "writer" "setreadpos" "setwritepos"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      close {
        eg "CLOSE filename"
        desc "command. Closes the named file. If the file was currently the reader or writer, then the reader or writer is changed to the keyboard or screen, as if SETREAD [] or SETWRITE [] had been done."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      allopen {
        eg "ALLOPEN"
        desc "outputs a list whose members are the names of all files currently open. This list does not include the dribble file, if any."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      closeall {
        eg "CLOSEALL  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Closes all open files. Abbreviates FOREACH ALLOPEN [CLOSE ?]"
        see "foreach" "close"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      erasefile {
        eg """
        ERASEFILE filename
        ERF filename
        """
        desc "command. Erases (deletes, removes) the named file, which should not currently be open."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      dribble {
        eg "DRIBBLE filename"
        desc "command. Creates a new file whose name is the input, like OPENWRITE, and begins recording in that file everything that is read from the keyboard or written to the terminal. That is, this writing is in addition to the writing to WRITER. The intent is to create a transcript of a Logo session, including things like prompt characters and interactions."
        see "openwrite" "writer"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it records what happens in a terminal. You can copy the console's text instead."
      }
      nodribble {
        eg "NODRIBBLE"
        desc "command. Stops copying information into the dribble file, and closes the file."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it records what happens in a terminal. You can copy the console's text instead."
      }
      setread {
        eg "SETREAD filename"
        desc "command. Makes the named file the read stream, used for READLIST, etc. The file must already be open with OPENREAD or OPENUPDATE. If the input is the empty list, then the read stream becomes the keyboard, as usual. Changing the read stream does not close the file that was previously the read stream, so it is possible to alternate between files."
        see "readlist" "openread" "openupdate"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      setwrite {
        eg "SETWRITE filename"
        desc """
        command. Makes the named file the write stream, used for PRINT, etc. The file must already be open with OPENWRITE, OPENAPPEND, or OPENUPDATE. If the input is the empty list, then the write stream becomes the screen, as usual. Changing the write stream does not close the file that was previously the write stream, so it is possible to alternate between files.

        If the input is a list, then its first element must be a variable name, and its second and last element must be a positive integer; a buffer of that many characters will be allocated, and will become the writestream. If the same list (same in the .EQ sense, not a copy) has been used as input to OPENWRITE, then the already-allocated buffer will be used, and the writer can be changed to and from this buffer, with all the characters accumulated as in a file. When the same list is used as input to CLOSE, the contents of the buffer (as an unparsed word, which may contain newline characters) will become the value of the named variable. For compatibility with earlier versions, if the list has not been opened when the SETWRITE is done, it will be opened implicitly, but the first SETWRITE after this one will implicitly close it, setting the variable and freeing the allocated buffer.
        """
        see "print" "openwrite" "openappend" "openupdate"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      reader {
        eg "READER"
        desc "outputs the name of the current read stream file, or the empty list if the read stream is the terminal."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      writer {
        eg "WRITER"
        desc "outputs the name of the current write stream file, or the empty list if the write stream is the screen."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      setreadpos {
        eg "SETREADPOS charpos"
        desc "command. Sets the file pointer of the read stream file so that the next READLIST, etc., will begin reading at the charposth character in the file, counting from 0. (That is, SETREADPOS 0 will start reading from the beginning of the file.) Meaningless if the read stream is the keyboard."
        see "readlist"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      setwritepos {
        eg "SETWRITEPOS charpos"
        desc "command. Sets the file pointer of the write stream file so that the next PRINT, etc., will begin writing at the charposth character in the file, counting from 0. (That is, SETWRITEPOS 0 will start writing from the beginning of the file.) Meaningless if the write stream is the screen."
        see "print"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      readpos {
        eg "READPOS"
        desc "outputs the file position of the current read stream file."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      writepos {
        eg "WRITEPOS"
        desc "outputs the file position of the current write stream file."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      eofp {
        eg """
        EOFP
        EOF?
        """
        desc "predicate, outputs TRUE if there are no more characters to be read in the read stream file, FALSE otherwise."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      filep {
        eg """
        FILEP filename
        FILE? filename  (library procedure)
        """
        desc "predicate, outputs TRUE if a file of the specified name exists and can be read, FALSE otherwise."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    terminal-access {
      keyp {
        eg """
        KEYP
        KEY?
        """
        desc "predicate, outputs TRUE if there are characters waiting to be read from the read stream. If the read stream is a file, this is equivalent to NOT EOFP. If the read stream is the terminal, then echoing is turned off and the terminal is set to cbreak (character at a time instead of line at a time) mode. It remains in this mode until some line-mode reading is requested (e.g., READLIST). The Unix operating system forgets about any pending characters when it switches modes, so the first KEYP invocation will always output FALSE."
        see "eofp" "readlist"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      linep {
        eg """
        LINEP
        LINE?
        """
        desc "predicate, outputs TRUE if there is a line waiting to be read from the read stream. If the read stream is a file, this is equivalent to NOT EOFP. If the read stream is the terminal, then typed characters will be displayed and may be edited until RET is pressed."
        see "eofp"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      cleartext {
        eg """
        CLEARTEXT
        CT
        """
        desc "command. Clears the text window."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      setcursor {
        eg "SETCURSOR vector"
        desc "command. The input is a list of two numbers, the x and y coordinates of a text window position (origin in the upper left corner, positive direction is southeast). The text cursor is moved to the requested position. This command also forces the immediate printing of any buffered characters."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it moves the cursor in a terminal, and Pendown's console is a scrolling list of lines."
      }
      cursor {
        eg "CURSOR"
        desc "outputs a list containing the current x and y coordinates of the text cursor. Logo may get confused about the current cursor position if, e.g., you type in a long line that wraps around or your program prints escape codes that affect the screen strangely."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it moves the cursor in a terminal, and Pendown's console is a scrolling list of lines."
      }
      setmargins {
        eg "SETMARGINS vector"
        desc "command. The input must be a list of two numbers, as for SETCURSOR. The effect is to clear the screen and then arrange for all further printing to be shifted down and to the right according to the indicated margins. Specifically, every time a newline character is printed (explicitly or implicitly) Logo will type x_margin spaces, and on every invocation of SETCURSOR the margins will be added to the input x and y coordinates. (CURSOR will report the cursor position relative to the margins, so that this shift will be invisible to Logo programs.) The purpose of this command is to accommodate the display of terminal screens in lecture halls with inadequate TV monitors that miss the top and left edges of the screen."
        see "setcursor"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it adds margins for projecting a terminal onto a wall."
      }
      settextcolor {
        eg """
        SETTEXTCOLOR foreground background
        SETTC foreground background
        """
        desc """
        command (wxWidgets only). The inputs are color numbers, or RGB color lists, as for turtle graphics. The foreground and background colors for the textscreen/splitscreen text window are changed to the given values. The change affects text already printed as well as future text printing; there is only one text color for the entire window.

        Command (non-wxWidgets Windows and DOS extended only). The inputs are color numbers, as for turtle graphics. Future printing to the text window will use the specified colors for foreground (the characters printed) and background (the space under those characters). Using STANDOUT will revert to the default text window colors. In the DOS extended (ucblogo.exe) version, colors in textscreen mode are limited to numbers 0-7, and the coloring applies only to text printed by the program, not to the echoing of text typed by the user. Neither limitation applies to the text portion of splitscreen mode, which is actually drawn as graphics internally.
        """
        see "standout"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "the console's font and colours are Pendown's settings, not something a program changes."
      }
      increasefont {
        eg """
        INCREASEFONT
        DECREASEFONT
        """
        desc "command (wxWidgets only). Increase or decrease the size of the font used in the text and edit windows to the next larger or smaller available size."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "the console's font and colours are Pendown's settings, not something a program changes."
      }
      settextsize {
        eg "SETTEXTSIZE height"
        desc "command (wxWidgets only). Set the \"point size\" of the font used in the text and edit windows to the given integer input. The desired size may not be available, in which case the nearest available size will be used. Note: There is only a slight correlation between these integers and pixel sizes. Our rough estimate is that the number of pixels of height is about 1.5 times the point size, but it varies for different fonts. See SETLABELHEIGHT for a different approach used for the graphics window."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "the console's font and colours are Pendown's settings, not something a program changes."
      }
      textsize {
        eg "TEXTSIZE"
        desc "(wxWidgets only) outputs the \"point size\" of the font used in the text and edit windows. See SETTEXTSIZE for a discussion of font sizing. See LABELSIZE for a different approach used for the graphics window."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "the console's font and colours are Pendown's settings, not something a program changes."
      }
      setfont {
        eg "SETFONT fontname"
        desc "command (wxWidgets only). Set the font family used in all windows to the one named by the input. Try Courier or Monospace as likely possibilities. Not all computers have the same fonts installed. It’s a good idea to stick with monospace fonts (ones in which all characters have the same width)."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "the console's font and colours are Pendown's settings, not something a program changes."
      }
      font {
        eg "FONT"
        desc "(wxWidgets only) outputs the name of the font family used in all windows."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "the console's font and colours are Pendown's settings, not something a program changes."
      }
    }
  }
  arithmetic {
    numeric-operations {
      sum {
        eg """
        SUM num1 num2
        (SUM num1 num2 num3 ...)
        num1 + num2
        """
        desc "outputs the sum of its inputs."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs its inputs added together. `+` between two numbers does the same. Put the instruction in parentheses to add more than two numbers.
        """
        example title="Adding up" """
        PRINT SUM 3 4
        PRINT 3 + 4
        PRINT (SUM 1 2 3 4 5)
        """
        example title="Growing steps" """
        MAKE "length 10
        REPEAT 30 [FD :length RT 90 MAKE "length :length + 10]
        """
        see "difference" "product"
      }
      difference {
        eg """
        DIFFERENCE num1 num2
        num1 - num2
        """
        desc "outputs the difference of its inputs. Minus sign means infix difference in ambiguous contexts (when preceded by a complete expression), unless it is preceded by a space and followed by a nonspace. (See also MINUS.)"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the first input take away the second. `-` between two numbers does the same.

        A `-` with a space before it and none after, as in `FD -10` or `PRINT 5 -3`, is a minus sign on the number after it, not a take-away. Write `5 - 3` with spaces on both sides to take away.
        """
        example title="Taking away" """
        PRINT DIFFERENCE 10 4
        PRINT 10 - 4
        PRINT 10-4
        """
        see "sum" "minus"
      }
      minus {
        eg """
        MINUS num
        - num
        """
        desc """
        outputs the negative of its input. Minus sign means unary minus if the previous token is an infix operator or open parenthesis, or it is preceded by a space and followed by a nonspace. There is a difference in binding strength between the two forms:

            MINUS 3 + 4     means   -(3+4)
            - 3 + 4         means   (-3)+4
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs its input with its sign changed, so `MINUS 5` is `-5` and `MINUS -5` is `5`. `-` in front of a number or a `:variable` does the same.
        """
        example title="Changing the sign" """
        MAKE "n 30
        PRINT MINUS :n
        PRINT -:n
        RT 90 FD 100 FD MINUS 50
        """
        see "difference"
      }
      product {
        eg """
        PRODUCT num1 num2
        (PRODUCT num1 num2 num3 ...)
        num1 * num2
        """
        desc "outputs the product of its inputs."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs its inputs multiplied together. `*` between two numbers does the same. Put the instruction in parentheses to multiply more than two numbers.
        """
        example title="Multiplying" """
        PRINT PRODUCT 6 7
        PRINT 6 * 7
        PRINT (PRODUCT 2 3 4)
        """
        see "quotient" "power"
      }
      quotient {
        eg """
        QUOTIENT num1 num2
        (QUOTIENT num)
        num1 / num2
        """
        desc "outputs the quotient of its inputs. The quotient of two integers is an integer if and only if the dividend is a multiple of the divisor. (In other words, QUOTIENT 5 2 is 2.5, not 2, but QUOTIENT 4 2 is 2, not 2.0 — it does the right thing.) With a single input, QUOTIENT outputs the reciprocal of the input."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the first input divided by the second. `/` between two numbers does the same. The answer can have a fraction: `7 / 2` is `3.5`. With one input, in parentheses, it outputs 1 divided by it.
        """
        example title="Dividing" """
        PRINT 7 / 2
        PRINT QUOTIENT 12 4
        PRINT (QUOTIENT 4)
        """
        example title="Sharing a turn" """
        MAKE "sides 9
        REPEAT :sides [FD 80 RT 360 / :sides]
        """
        see "product" "remainder"
      }
      remainder {
        eg "REMAINDER num1 num2"
        desc "outputs the remainder on dividing num1 by num2; both must be integers and the result is an integer with the same sign as num1."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs what's left over when the first whole number is divided by the second. The answer has the same sign as the first number. See [MODULO] for one with the sign of the second.
        """
        example title="Left over" """
        PRINT REMAINDER 17 5
        PRINT REMAINDER -17 5
        """
        example title="Every third one red" """
        REPEAT 12 [
          IFELSE (REMAINDER REPCOUNT 3) = 0 [SETPC "red] [SETPC 7]
          FD 120 BK 120 RT 30
        ]
        """
        see "modulo" "quotient"
      }
      modulo {
        eg "MODULO num1 num2"
        desc "outputs the remainder on dividing num1 by num2; both must be integers and the result is an integer with the same sign as num2."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs what's left over when the first whole number is divided by the second, with the same sign as the second number. For positive numbers it's the same as [REMAINDER].
        """
        example title="Left over, the other way" """
        PRINT MODULO 17 5
        PRINT MODULO -17 5
        """
        see "remainder"
      }
      int {
        eg "INT num"
        desc "outputs its input with fractional part removed, i.e., an integer with the same sign as the input, whose absolute value is the largest integer less than or equal to the absolute value of the input."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the whole-number part of its input, dropping any fraction: `INT 3.7` is `3`, and `INT -3.7` is `-3`.
        """
        example title="Dropping the fraction" """
        PRINT INT 3.7
        PRINT INT -3.7
        """
        see "round"
      }
      round {
        eg "ROUND num"
        desc "outputs the nearest integer to the input."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the whole number nearest its input. A number exactly half way rounds away from zero, so `ROUND 2.5` is `3`.
        """
        example title="To the nearest" """
        PRINT ROUND 3.7
        PRINT ROUND 3.2
        PRINT ROUND 2.5
        """
        see "int"
      }
      sqrt {
        eg "SQRT num"
        desc "outputs the square root of the input, which must be nonnegative."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the square root of its input: the number that multiplied by itself gives the input. The input can't be negative.
        """
        example title="Square roots" """
        PRINT SQRT 16
        PRINT SQRT 2
        """
        example title="Across a square" """
        REPEAT 4 [FD 100 RT 90]
        RT 45 FD 100 * SQRT 2
        """
        see "power"
      }
      power {
        eg "POWER num1 num2"
        desc "outputs num1 to the num2 power. If num1 is negative, then num2 must be an integer."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the first input multiplied by itself the second input's number of times: `POWER 2 3` is 2 × 2 × 2, `8`. If the first input is negative, the second must be a whole number.
        """
        example title="Powers of two" """
        SHOW MAP [POWER 2 ?] ISEQ 1 8
        """
        example title="Doubling" """
        REPEAT 7 [FD POWER 2 REPCOUNT RT 90]
        """
        see "sqrt" "exp"
      }
      exp {
        eg "EXP num"
        desc "outputs e (2.718281828+) to the input power."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs `e`, about 2.718, to the power of its input.
        """
        example title="Powers of e" """
        PRINT EXP 1
        PRINT EXP 2
        """
        see "ln" "power"
      }
      log10 {
        eg "LOG10 num"
        desc "outputs the common logarithm of the input."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the logarithm of its input to base 10: the power 10 must be raised to, to give it. `LOG10 1000` is `3`.
        """
        example title="Powers of ten" """
        PRINT LOG10 1000
        PRINT LOG10 2
        """
        see "ln"
      }
      ln {
        eg "LN num"
        desc "outputs the natural logarithm of the input."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the natural logarithm of its input: the power `e` must be raised to, to give it.
        """
        example title="Natural logs" """
        PRINT LN EXP 3
        PRINT LN 10
        """
        see "exp" "log10"
      }
      sin {
        eg "SIN degrees"
        desc "outputs the sine of its input, which is taken in degrees."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the sine of an angle in degrees. With [COS], it works out where a point on a circle is.
        """
        example title="A sine wave" """
        PU SETXY -180 0 PD
        REPEAT 72 [SETXY REPCOUNT * 5 - 180 100 * SIN REPCOUNT * 10]
        """
        example title="Points on a circle" """
        REPEAT 12 [
          PU SETXY 150 * COS REPCOUNT * 30 150 * SIN REPCOUNT * 30 PD
          CIRCLE 10
        ]
        """
        see "cos" "radsin" "arctan"
      }
      radsin {
        eg "RADSIN radians"
        desc "outputs the sine of its input, which is taken in radians."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the sine of an angle in radians, where a whole turn is `2π`, about 6.283. [SIN] takes degrees.
        """
        example title="Radians" """
        PRINT RADSIN 0
        PRINT RADSIN 3.14159265358979 / 2
        """
        see "sin" "radcos"
      }
      cos {
        eg "COS degrees"
        desc "outputs the cosine of its input, which is taken in degrees."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the cosine of an angle in degrees. With [SIN], it works out where a point on a circle is.
        """
        example title="A cosine wave" """
        PU SETXY -180 100 PD
        REPEAT 72 [SETXY REPCOUNT * 5 - 180 100 * COS REPCOUNT * 10]
        """
        see "sin" "radcos"
      }
      radcos {
        eg "RADCOS radians"
        desc "outputs the cosine of its input, which is taken in radians."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the cosine of an angle in radians, where a whole turn is `2π`, about 6.283. [COS] takes degrees.
        """
        example title="Radians" """
        PRINT RADCOS 0
        PRINT RADCOS 3.14159265358979
        """
        see "cos" "radsin"
      }
      arctan {
        eg """
        ARCTAN num
        (ARCTAN x y)
        """
        desc "outputs the arctangent, in degrees, of its input. With two inputs, outputs the arctangent of y/x, if x is nonzero, or 90 or –90 depending on the sign of y, if x is zero."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the angle, in degrees, whose tangent is its input. With two inputs, in parentheses, `(ARCTAN x y)` outputs the angle of the line from `[0 0]` to `[x y]`, measured from the `x` direction.
        """
        example title="Angles" """
        PRINT ARCTAN 1
        PRINT (ARCTAN 0 1)
        PRINT (ARCTAN -1 0)
        """
        see "towards" "radarctan"
      }
      radarctan {
        eg """
        RADARCTAN num
        (RADARCTAN x y)
        """
        desc """
        outputs the arctangent, in radians, of its input. With two inputs, outputs the arctangent of y/x, if x is nonzero, or pi/2 or –pi/2 depending on the sign of y, if x is zero.

        The expression 2*(RADARCTAN 0 1) can be used to get the value of pi.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Like [ARCTAN], but outputs the angle in radians, where a whole turn is `2π`. `2 * (RADARCTAN 0 1)` is `π`.
        """
        example title="Pi" """
        PRINT 2 * (RADARCTAN 0 1)
        """
        see "arctan"
      }
      iseq {
        eg "ISEQ from to  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        outputs a list of the integers from from to to, inclusive.

            ? show iseq 3 7
            [3 4 5 6 7]
            ? show iseq 7 3
            [7 6 5 4 3]
        """
        differs "Refuses to make more than 10 million members, so a typo such as ISEQ 1 1e12 is an error instead of running out of memory."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a list of the whole numbers from the first input to the second, counting up or down.
        """
        example title="Counting" """
        SHOW ISEQ 1 10
        SHOW ISEQ 5 1
        """
        example title="Each number a side" """
        FOREACH ISEQ 1 30 [FD ? * 5 RT 90]
        """
        see "rseq" "for"
      }
      rseq {
        eg "RSEQ from to count  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        outputs a list of count equally spaced rational numbers between from and to, inclusive.

            ? show rseq 3 5 9
            [3 3.25 3.5 3.75 4 4.25 4.5 4.75 5]
            ? show rseq 3 5 5
            [3 3.5 4 4.5 5]
        """
        differs "Refuses to make more than 10 million members, so a typo such as ISEQ 1 1e12 is an error instead of running out of memory."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a list of numbers evenly spaced from the first input to the second. The third input says how many there are.
        """
        example title="Evenly spaced" """
        SHOW RSEQ 0 1 5
        SHOW RSEQ 10 -10 3
        """
        see "iseq"
      }
    }
    numeric-predicates {
      lessp {
        eg """
        LESSP num1 num2
        LESS? num1 num2
        num1 < num2
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if its first input is strictly less than its second."
        differs "Numbers are compared rounded to 15 significant digits, as EQUALP does, so this always agrees with =."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the first number is less than the second, and `FALSE` if it isn't. `<` between two numbers does the same.
        """
        example title="Smaller?" """
        PRINT 3 < 5
        PRINT LESSP 5 3
        """
        example title="Growing until big enough" """
        MAKE "size 10
        WHILE [:size < 200] [SQUARE :size MAKE "size :size * 1.5]
        """
        see "greaterp" "lessequalp"
      }
      greaterp {
        eg """
        GREATERP num1 num2
        GREATER? num1 num2
        num1 > num2
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if its first input is strictly greater than its second."
        differs "Numbers are compared rounded to 15 significant digits, as EQUALP does, so this always agrees with =."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the first number is greater than the second, and `FALSE` if it isn't. `>` between two numbers does the same.
        """
        example title="Bigger?" """
        PRINT 5 > 3
        PRINT GREATERP 3 5
        """
        see "lessp" "greaterequalp"
      }
      lessequalp {
        eg """
        LESSEQUALP num1 num2
        LESSEQUAL? num1 num2
        num1 <= num2
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if its first input is less than or equal to its second."
        differs "Numbers are compared rounded to 15 significant digits, as EQUALP does, so this always agrees with =."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the first number is less than or equal to the second. `<=` between two numbers does the same.
        """
        example title="Up to" """
        PRINT 3 <= 3
        PRINT 4 <= 3
        """
        see "lessp" "greaterequalp"
      }
      greaterequalp {
        eg """
        GREATEREQUALP num1 num2
        GREATEREQUAL? num1 num2
        num1 >= num2
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if its first input is greater than or equal to its second."
        differs "Numbers are compared rounded to 15 significant digits, as EQUALP does, so this always agrees with =."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the first number is greater than or equal to the second. `>=` between two numbers does the same.
        """
        example title="At least" """
        PRINT 3 >= 3
        PRINT 2 >= 3
        """
        see "greaterp" "lessequalp"
      }
    }
    random-numbers {
      random {
        eg """
        RANDOM num
        (RANDOM start end)
        """
        desc """
        with one input, outputs a random nonnegative integer less than its input, which must be a positive integer.

        With two inputs, RANDOM outputs a random integer greater than or equal to the first input, and less than or equal to the second input. Both inputs must be integers, and the first must be less than the second. (RANDOM 0 9) is equivalent to RANDOM 10; (RANDOM 3 8) is equivalent to (RANDOM 6)+3.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs a whole number chosen at random, from 0 up to one less than its input, so `RANDOM 6` is 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 or 5. With two inputs, in parentheses, it's from the first to the second, so `(RANDOM 1 6)` rolls a dice.
        """
        example title="Rolling dice" """
        REPEAT 5 [PRINT (RANDOM 1 6)]
        """
        example title="Scattered stars" """
        SETPC "yellow
        HT
        REPEAT 40 [
          PU SETXY (RANDOM -400 400) (RANDOM -400 400) PD
          REPEAT 5 [FD 20 RT 144]
        ]
        """
        see "rerandom" "pick"
      }
      rerandom {
        eg """
        RERANDOM
        (RERANDOM seed)
        """
        desc "command. Makes the results of RANDOM reproducible. Ordinarily the sequence of random numbers is different each time Logo is used. If you need the same sequence of pseudo-random numbers repeatedly, e.g. to debug a program, say RERANDOM before the first invocation of RANDOM. If you need more than one repeatable sequence, you can give RERANDOM an integer input; each possible input selects a unique sequence of numbers."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Starts the random numbers from the beginning again, so [RANDOM] gives the same numbers as last time. It's useful when you want a random drawing you can draw again. Give a number, in parentheses, to pick a different sequence.
        """
        example title="The same random numbers" """
        (RERANDOM 7)
        REPEAT 5 [TYPE RANDOM 10]
        PRINT "
        (RERANDOM 7)
        REPEAT 5 [TYPE RANDOM 10]
        PRINT "
        """
        see "random"
      }
    }
    print-formatting {
      form {
        eg "FORM num width precision"
        desc """
        outputs a word containing a printable representation of num, possibly preceded by spaces (and therefore not a number for purposes of performing arithmetic operations), with at least width characters, including exactly precision digits after the decimal point. (If precision is 0 then there will be no decimal point in the output.)

        As a debugging feature, (FORM num -1 format) will print the floating point num according to the C printf format, to allow

            to hex :num
            op form :num -1 "|%08X %08X|
            end

        to allow finding out the exact result of floating point operations. The precise format needed may be machine-dependent.
        """
        differs "(FORM num -1 format), with a printf format, isn't supported."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a number as a word with exactly `precision` digits after the decimal point, padded with spaces in front to be at least `width` characters long. It's useful for lining numbers up in columns.
        """
        example title="Lining up" """
        PRINT FORM 3.14159 8 2
        PRINT FORM 42 8 2
        PRINT FORM 2 / 3 1 4
        """
      }
    }
    bitwise-operations {
      bitand {
        eg """
        BITAND num1 num2
        (BITAND num1 num2 num3 ...)
        """
        desc "outputs the bitwise and of its inputs, which must be integers."
        see "and"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      bitor {
        eg """
        BITOR num1 num2
        (BITOR num1 num2 num3 ...)
        """
        desc "outputs the bitwise or of its inputs, which must be integers."
        see "or"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      bitxor {
        eg """
        BITXOR num1 num2
        (BITXOR num1 num2 num3 ...)
        """
        desc "outputs the bitwise exclusive or of its inputs, which must be integers."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      bitnot {
        eg "BITNOT num"
        desc "outputs the bitwise not of its input, which must be an integer."
        see "not"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      ashift {
        eg "ASHIFT num1 num2"
        desc "outputs num1 arithmetic-shifted to the left by num2 bits. If num2 is negative, the shift is to the right with sign extension. The inputs must be integers."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      lshift {
        eg "LSHIFT num1 num2"
        desc "outputs num1 logical-shifted to the left by num2 bits. If num2 is negative, the shift is to the right with zero fill. The inputs must be integers."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
  }
  logical-operations {
    and {
      eg """
      AND tf1 tf2
      (AND tf1 tf2 tf3 ...)
      """
      desc """
      outputs TRUE if all inputs are TRUE, otherwise FALSE. All inputs must be TRUE or FALSE. (Comparison is case-insensitive regardless of the value of CASEIGNOREDP. That is, true or True or TRUE are all the same.) An input can be a list, in which case it is taken as an expression to run; that expression must produce a TRUE or FALSE value. List expressions are evaluated from left to right; as soon as a FALSE value is found, the remaining inputs are not examined. Example:

          MAKE "RESULT AND [NOT (:X = 0)] [(1 / :X) > .5]

      to avoid the division by zero if the first part is false.
      """
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 1
      help """
      Outputs `TRUE` if all of its inputs are `TRUE`, and `FALSE` otherwise. Put the instruction in parentheses for more than two inputs.

      An input can be a list holding a question, such as `[:x > 0]`. Lists are worked out left to right, and only until one is `FALSE`, so a later one that would be an error isn't run.
      """
      example title="Both true" """
      MAKE "x 5
      PRINT AND :x > 0 :x < 10
      PRINT AND :x > 0 :x > 10
      """
      example title="Only if it's safe" """
      MAKE "n 0
      PRINT AND [:n <> 0] [(10 / :n) > 1]
      """
      see "or" "not" "if"
    }
    or {
      eg """
      OR tf1 tf2
      (OR tf1 tf2 tf3 ...)
      """
      desc """
      outputs TRUE if any input is TRUE, otherwise FALSE. All inputs must be TRUE or FALSE. (Comparison is case-insensitive regardless of the value of CASEIGNOREDP. That is, true or True or TRUE are all the same.) An input can be a list, in which case it is taken as an expression to run; that expression must produce a TRUE or FALSE value. List expressions are evaluated from left to right; as soon as a TRUE value is found, the remaining inputs are not examined. Example:

          IF OR :X=0 [some.long.computation] [...]

      to avoid the long computation if the first condition is met.
      """
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 1
      help """
      Outputs `TRUE` if any of its inputs is `TRUE`, and `FALSE` if none are. Put the instruction in parentheses for more than two inputs.

      An input can be a list holding a question. Lists are worked out left to right, and only until one is `TRUE`.
      """
      example title="Either one" """
      MAKE "colour "red
      PRINT OR :colour = "red :colour = "orange
      PRINT (OR "false "false "false)
      """
      see "and" "not"
    }
    not {
      eg "NOT tf"
      desc "outputs TRUE if the input is FALSE, and vice versa. The input can be a list, in which case it is taken as an expression to run; that expression must produce a TRUE or FALSE value."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 1
      help """
      Outputs `TRUE` if its input is `FALSE`, and `FALSE` if it's `TRUE`.
      """
      example title="The opposite" """
      PRINT NOT "true
      PRINT NOT 3 > 5
      """
      see "and" "or"
    }
  }
  graphics {
    turtle-motion {
      forward {
        eg """
        FORWARD dist
        FD dist
        """
        desc "moves the turtle forward, in the direction that it’s facing, by the specified distance (measured in turtle steps)."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Kame moves forward by `distance` turtle steps, in the direction it's facing. If the pen is down, Kame draws a line as it goes.

        A negative distance moves Kame backward, as [BACK] does. At the edge of the drawing area, Kame wraps round to the other side, stops, or carries on out of sight, depending on [WRAP], [FENCE] and [WINDOW].
        """
        example title="A staircase" """
        REPEAT 4 [FD 40 RT 90 FD 40 LT 90]
        """
        example title="A dashed line" """
        REPEAT 5 [FD 20 PU FD 20 PD]
        """
        see "back" "left" "right" "penup"
      }
      back {
        eg """
        BACK dist
        BK dist
        """
        desc "moves the turtle backward, i.e., exactly opposite to the direction that it’s facing, by the specified distance. (The heading of the turtle does not change.)"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Kame moves backward by `distance` turtle steps, without turning round: it keeps facing the same way. If the pen is down, Kame draws a line as it goes.
        """
        example title="Out and back" """
        REPEAT 8 [FD 150 BK 150 RT 45]
        """
        see "forward"
      }
      left {
        eg """
        LEFT degrees
        LT degrees
        """
        desc "turns the turtle counterclockwise by the specified angle, measured in degrees (1/360 of a circle)."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Kame turns to its left by `degrees`, staying where it is. A whole turn is 360 degrees, so `LT 90` is a quarter turn.
        """
        example title="Turning left" """
        REPEAT 6 [FD 100 LT 60]
        """
        see "right" "setheading"
      }
      right {
        eg """
        RIGHT degrees
        RT degrees
        """
        desc "turns the turtle clockwise by the specified angle, measured in degrees (1/360 of a circle)."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Kame turns to its right by `degrees`, staying where it is. A whole turn is 360 degrees, so `RT 90` is a quarter turn.

        If Kame turns a whole turn while it draws a closed shape, the shape ends where it started: 4 turns of 90, or 5 of 72.
        """
        example title="A square" """
        REPEAT 4 [FD 150 RT 90]
        """
        example title="A star" """
        REPEAT 5 [FD 200 RT 144]
        """
        see "left" "setheading"
      }
      setpos {
        eg "SETPOS pos"
        desc "moves the turtle to an absolute position in the graphics window. The input is a list of two numbers, the X and Y coordinates."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Moves Kame straight to a position, given as a list of two numbers, `[x y]`. The middle of the drawing area is `[0 0]`, `x` goes up to the right, and `y` goes up towards the top. Kame keeps facing the same way, and draws a line if the pen is down.
        """
        example title="Joining the dots" """
        SETPOS [100 0]
        SETPOS [0 150]
        SETPOS [-100 0]
        SETPOS [0 0]
        """
        see "setxy" "pos" "home"
      }
      setxy {
        eg "SETXY xcor ycor"
        desc "moves the turtle to an absolute position in the graphics window. The two inputs are numbers, the X and Y coordinates."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Moves Kame straight to the position `x`, `y`, like [SETPOS] but with two inputs instead of a list. Kame draws a line if the pen is down.
        """
        example title="A zigzag" """
        PU SETXY -150 0 PD
        REPEAT 7 [SETXY REPCOUNT * 40 - 150 60 * MODULO REPCOUNT 2]
        """
        see "setpos" "setx" "sety"
      }
      setx {
        eg "SETX xcor"
        desc "moves the turtle horizontally from its old position to a new absolute horizontal coordinate. The input is the new X coordinate."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Moves Kame straight across, left or right, to a new `x` position. Its `y` position stays the same.
        """
        example title="Across and back" """
        SETX 150
        SETX -150
        """
        see "sety" "setxy"
      }
      sety {
        eg "SETY ycor"
        desc "moves the turtle vertically from its old position to a new absolute vertical coordinate. The input is the new Y coordinate."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Moves Kame straight up or down to a new `y` position. Its `x` position stays the same.
        """
        example title="Up and down" """
        SETY 150
        SETY -150
        """
        see "setx" "setxy"
      }
      setheading {
        eg """
        SETHEADING degrees
        SETH degrees
        """
        desc "turns the turtle to a new absolute heading. The input is a number, the heading in degrees clockwise from the positive Y axis."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Turns Kame to face a heading, in degrees: 0 is straight up, 90 is to the right, 180 is down and 270 is to the left. Unlike [RIGHT] and [LEFT], it doesn't matter which way Kame was facing before.
        """
        example title="Every heading" """
        REPEAT 12 [SETH REPCOUNT * 30 FD 120 BK 120]
        """
        see "heading" "right" "towards"
      }
      home {
        eg "HOME"
        desc "moves the turtle to the center of the screen. Equivalent to SETPOS [0 0] SETHEADING 0."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Brings Kame back to the middle of the drawing area, `[0 0]`, facing straight up. It draws a line on the way if the pen is down.
        """
        example title="A triangle home" """
        FD 150 RT 120 FD 150
        HOME
        """
        see "clearscreen" "setpos"
      }
      arc {
        eg "ARC angle radius"
        desc "draws an arc of a circle, with the turtle at the center, with the specified radius, starting at the turtle’s heading and extending clockwise through the specified angle. The turtle does not move."
        differs """
        The turtle walks along the arc instead of drawing around itself. It moves along an arc of a circle with the given radius, turning through the given angle as it goes: a positive angle curves right (clockwise, like RT) and a negative angle curves left. It finishes at the end of the arc with its heading changed by the angle, so ARC 360 50 draws a full circle and returns to where it started, and REPEAT 4 [ARC 90 50] does the same. It draws only when the pen is down, and wraps or stops at the edges like FD. Use CIRCLE to draw a circle around the turtle.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Kame walks along part of a circle with this `radius`, turning through `angle` degrees as it goes, and draws it if the pen is down. A positive angle curves to the right, like [RIGHT], and a negative one to the left.

        Kame ends at the end of the arc, with its heading changed by the angle, so `ARC 360 50` draws a whole circle and comes back to where it started. This isn't UCBLogo's `ARC`, which draws around the turtle without moving it: for that, use [CIRCLE].
        """
        example title="A wave" """
        RT 90
        REPEAT 4 [ARC -180 30 ARC 180 30]
        """
        example title="Petals" """
        REPEAT 6 [ARC 90 100 RT 90 ARC 90 100 RT 30]
        """
        see "circle" "forward"
      }
    }
    shapes {
      circle {
        eg "CIRCLE radius"
        desc "draws a circle with the given radius, centred on the turtle. The turtle does not move. The outline is drawn with the current pen if the pen is down, and the circle is filled if a fill colour is set (see SETFILLCOLOR), even when the pen is up."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        extension #true
        source "LibreLogo, FMSLogo"
        help """
        Draws a circle with this `radius`, with Kame at its centre. Kame doesn't move.

        The outline is drawn with the pen if it's down. If a fill colour is set with [SETFILLCOLOR], the circle is filled with it, even when the pen is up.
        """
        example title="Rings" """
        REPEAT 5 [CIRCLE REPCOUNT * 30]
        """
        example title="Filled circles" """
        SETFC "gold
        CIRCLE 120
        SETFC "orangered
        PU
        CIRCLE 60
        """
        see "ellipse" "arc" "setfillcolor"
      }
      ellipse {
        eg "ELLIPSE across along"
        desc "draws an ellipse centred on the turtle, turned to match the turtle's heading. The first input is the radius from side to side relative to the turtle, and the second is the radius in the direction the turtle is heading, so at heading 0 they're the X and Y radii. The turtle does not move. The outline is drawn with the current pen if the pen is down, and the ellipse is filled if a fill colour is set (see SETFILLCOLOR), even when the pen is up."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        extension #true
        source "LibreLogo, FMSLogo"
        help """
        Draws an oval centred on Kame, turned to match Kame's heading. The first input is how far it reaches to each side of Kame, and the second how far it reaches ahead of and behind Kame. Kame doesn't move.

        The outline is drawn with the pen if it's down, and the oval is filled if a fill colour is set with [SETFILLCOLOR], even when the pen is up.
        """
        example title="An egg" """
        ELLIPSE 80 120
        """
        example title="A flower of ovals" """
        SETFC [255 120 180 120]
        REPEAT 6 [ELLIPSE 30 120 RT 30]
        """
        see "circle" "rectangle" "setfillcolor"
      }
      square {
        eg """
        SQUARE side
        (SQUARE side corner)
        """
        desc "draws a square with sides of the given length, centred on the turtle and turned to match the turtle's heading. With the optional third input, the corners are rounded with that radius. The turtle does not move. The outline is drawn with the current pen if the pen is down, and the square is filled if a fill colour is set (see SETFILLCOLOR), even when the pen is up."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        extension #true
        source "LibreLogo"
        help """
        Draws a square with sides of this length, centred on Kame and turned to match Kame's heading. Kame doesn't move. With a second input, in parentheses, the corners are rounded with that radius.

        The outline is drawn with the pen if it's down, and the square is filled if a fill colour is set with [SETFILLCOLOR], even when the pen is up.
        """
        example title="Turning squares" """
        REPEAT 6 [SQUARE 200 RT 15]
        """
        example title="Rounded corners" """
        SETFC "teal
        (SQUARE 200 40)
        """
        see "rectangle" "setfillcolor"
      }
      rectangle {
        eg """
        RECTANGLE across along
        (RECTANGLE across along corner)
        """
        desc "draws a rectangle centred on the turtle and turned to match the turtle's heading. The first input is its full width from side to side relative to the turtle, and the second is its full length in the direction the turtle is heading, so at heading 0 they're the width and height. With the optional third input, the corners are rounded with that radius. The turtle does not move. The outline is drawn with the current pen if the pen is down, and the rectangle is filled if a fill colour is set (see SETFILLCOLOR), even when the pen is up."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        extension #true
        source "LibreLogo"
        help """
        Draws a rectangle centred on Kame and turned to match Kame's heading. The first input is its width from side to side, and the second its length in the direction Kame is facing. Kame doesn't move. With a third input, in parentheses, the corners are rounded with that radius.

        The outline is drawn with the pen if it's down, and the rectangle is filled if a fill colour is set with [SETFILLCOLOR], even when the pen is up.
        """
        example title="A door" """
        SETFC "sienna
        RECTANGLE 120 220
        PU SETXY 35 0
        SETFC "gold
        CIRCLE 8
        """
        example title="A rounded button" """
        (RECTANGLE 260 100 30)
        """
        see "square" "ellipse" "setfillcolor"
      }
    }
    turtle-motion-queries {
      pos {
        eg "POS"
        desc "outputs the turtle’s current position, as a list of two numbers, the X and Y coordinates."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs Kame's position as a list of two numbers, `[x y]`. The middle of the drawing area is `[0 0]`.
        """
        example title="Where is Kame?" """
        FD 100 RT 90 FD 50
        SHOW POS
        """
        see "setpos" "xcor" "ycor"
      }
      xcor {
        eg "XCOR  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs a number, the turtle’s X coordinate."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs Kame's `x` position: how far it is to the right of the middle, or to the left if it's negative.
        """
        example title="Across" """
        RT 90 FD 120
        PRINT XCOR
        """
        see "ycor" "pos"
      }
      ycor {
        eg "YCOR  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs a number, the turtle’s Y coordinate."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs Kame's `y` position: how far it is above the middle, or below if it's negative.
        """
        example title="Up" """
        FD 75
        PRINT YCOR
        """
        see "xcor" "pos"
      }
      heading {
        eg "HEADING"
        desc "outputs a number, the turtle’s heading in degrees."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs which way Kame is facing, in degrees: 0 is straight up, 90 is to the right, 180 is down and 270 is to the left.
        """
        example title="Which way?" """
        RT 45 RT 45
        PRINT HEADING
        LT 135
        PRINT HEADING
        """
        see "setheading" "towards"
      }
      towards {
        eg "TOWARDS pos"
        desc "outputs a number, the heading at which the turtle should be facing so that it would point from its current position to the position given as the input."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the heading Kame would need to face a position, given as `[x y]`. Use it with [SETHEADING] to point Kame at something.
        """
        example title="Pointing at a spot" """
        PU SETPOS [-150 -100] PD
        SETH TOWARDS [100 120]
        FD DISTANCE.TO [100 120]
        TO DISTANCE.TO :p
          OUTPUT SQRT (POWER (FIRST :p) - XCOR 2) + (POWER (LAST :p) - YCOR 2)
        END
        """
        see "setheading" "pos"
      }
      scrunch {
        eg "SCRUNCH"
        desc "outputs a list containing two numbers, the X and Y scrunch factors, as used by SETSCRUNCH. (But note that SETSCRUNCH takes two numbers as inputs, not one list of numbers.)"
        see "setscrunch"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it corrected the shape of old screens, whose pixels weren't square."
      }
    }
    turtle-and-window-control {
      showturtle {
        eg """
        SHOWTURTLE
        ST
        """
        desc "makes the turtle visible."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Shows Kame again after [HIDETURTLE]. Kame is shown to start with.
        """
        example title="Now you see it" """
        HT
        FD 100
        ST
        """
        see "hideturtle" "shownp"
      }
      hideturtle {
        eg """
        HIDETURTLE
        HT
        """
        desc "makes the turtle invisible. It’s a good idea to do this while you’re in the middle of a complicated drawing, because hiding the turtle speeds up the drawing substantially."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Hides Kame, so only the drawing shows. Kame still moves and draws; you just can't see it.
        """
        example title="Just the drawing" """
        HT
        REPEAT 3 [FD 150 RT 120]
        """
        see "showturtle" "shownp"
      }
      clean {
        eg "CLEAN"
        desc "erases all lines that the turtle has drawn on the graphics window. The turtle’s state (position, heading, pen mode, etc.) is not changed."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Wipes the drawing, but leaves Kame where it is, facing the same way. The background colour stays too.
        """
        example title="Wiping the slate" """
        REPEAT 4 [FD 100 RT 90]
        CLEAN
        CIRCLE 50
        """
        see "clearscreen"
      }
      clearscreen {
        eg """
        CLEARSCREEN
        CS
        """
        desc "erases the graphics window and sends the turtle to its initial position and heading. Like HOME and CLEAN together."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Wipes the drawing and takes Kame back to the middle, facing up, like [CLEAN] and [HOME] together. Kame doesn't draw a line on the way home.
        """
        example title="Starting again" """
        RT 45 FD 150
        CS
        REPEAT 3 [FD 100 RT 120]
        """
        see "clean" "home"
      }
      wrap {
        eg "WRAP"
        desc "tells the turtle to enter wrap mode: From now on, if the turtle is asked to move past the boundary of the graphics window, it will \"wrap around\" and reappear at the opposite edge of the window. The top edge wraps to the bottom edge, while the left edge wraps to the right edge. (So the window is topologically equivalent to a torus.) This is the turtle’s initial mode. Compare WINDOW and FENCE."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        When Kame goes off one edge of the drawing area, it comes back on at the opposite edge, and carries on. This is how Kame starts.
        """
        example title="Off the edge and back" """
        WRAP
        RT 30
        FD 1500
        """
        see "window" "fence" "turtlemode"
      }
      window {
        eg "WINDOW"
        desc "tells the turtle to enter window mode: From now on, if the turtle is asked to move past the boundary of the graphics window, it will move offscreen. The visible graphics window is considered as just part of an infinite graphics plane; the turtle can be anywhere on the plane. (If you lose the turtle, HOME will bring it back to the center of the window.) Compare WRAP and FENCE."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Kame can go off the edge of the drawing area and carry on out of sight, as if the area were a window onto a bigger page. [HOME] brings it back.
        """
        example title="Out of the window" """
        WINDOW
        RT 30
        FD 800
        PU HOME PD
        CIRCLE 50
        """
        see "wrap" "fence" "turtlemode"
      }
      fence {
        eg "FENCE"
        desc "tells the turtle to enter fence mode: From now on, if the turtle is asked to move past the boundary of the graphics window, it will move as far as it can and then stop at the edge with an \"out of bounds\" error message. Compare WRAP and WINDOW."
        differs "A move that would cross the fence is drawn up to the edge, and stops there with an error. That includes SETPOS, SETXY and the rest, which in UCBLogo give the error without moving at all."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Kame can't go past the edge of the drawing area: a move that would cross it stops at the edge, with an error.
        """
        example title="Stopping at the edge" """
        FENCE
        CATCH "ERROR [FD 1000]
        PRINT FIRST BUTFIRST ERROR
        """
        see "wrap" "window" "turtlemode"
      }
      fill {
        eg "FILL"
        desc "fills in a region of the graphics window containing the turtle and bounded by lines that have been drawn earlier. This is not portable; it doesn’t work for all machines, and may not work exactly the same way on different machines."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      filled {
        eg "FILLED color instructions"
        desc "runs the instructions, remembering all points visited by turtle motion commands, starting and ending with the turtle’s initial position. Then draws (ignoring penmode) the resulting polygon, in the current pen color, filling the polygon with the given color, which can be a color number or an RGB list. The instruction list cannot include another FILLED invocation. (wxWidgets only)"
        differs "The colour can be any of our colour forms: see SETPENCOLOR."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      label {
        eg "LABEL text"
        desc "takes a word or list as input, and prints the input on the graphics window, starting at the turtle’s position."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      setlabelheight {
        eg "SETLABELHEIGHT height"
        desc "command (wxWidgets only). Takes a positive integer argument and tries to set the font size so that the character height (including descenders) is that many turtle steps. This will be different from the number of screen pixels if SETSCRUNCH has been used. Also, note that SETSCRUNCH changes the font size to try to preserve this height in turtle steps. Note that the query operation corresponding to this command is LABELSIZE, not LABELHEIGHT, because it tells you the width as well as the height of characters in the current font."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      textscreen {
        eg """
        TEXTSCREEN
        TS
        """
        desc "rearranges the size and position of windows to maximize the space available in the text window (the window used for interaction with Logo). The details differ among machines. Compare SPLITSCREEN and FULLSCREEN."
        see "splitscreen"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it switched one screen between text and drawing, and Pendown always shows both."
      }
      fullscreen {
        eg """
        FULLSCREEN
        FS
        """
        desc """
        rearranges the size and position of windows to maximize the space available in the graphics window. The details differ among machines. Compare SPLITSCREEN and TEXTSCREEN.

        Since there must be a text window to allow printing (including the printing of the Logo prompt), Logo automatically switches from fullscreen to splitscreen whenever anything is printed.

        In the DOS version, switching from fullscreen to splitscreen loses the part of the picture that’s hidden by the text window. [This design decision follows from the scarcity of memory, so that the extra memory to remember an invisible part of a drawing seems too expensive.]
        """
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it switched one screen between text and drawing, and Pendown always shows both."
      }
      splitscreen {
        eg """
        SPLITSCREEN
        SS
        """
        desc "rearranges the size and position of windows to allow some room for text interaction while also keeping most of the graphics window visible. The details differ among machines. Compare TEXTSCREEN and FULLSCREEN."
        see "textscreen"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it switched one screen between text and drawing, and Pendown always shows both."
      }
      setspeed {
        eg "SETSPEED level"
        desc "sets how fast the turtle draws, as a level from 1 to 11. Level 11 is Instant: everything appears at once. Below it, each level is twice as fast as the one before, from 5 turtle steps a second at level 1 to 2,560 at level 10. The speed changes at exactly this point in the program, and the speed slider moves to match. The input must be a whole number from 1 to 11. When a picture is drawn with pendown-cli, SETSPEED does nothing."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        extension #true
        source "Python turtle.speed()"
        help """
        Sets how fast Kame draws, as a level from 1 to 11, and moves the speed slider to match. Level 1 is 5 steps a second, each level is twice as fast as the one before, and 11 is Instant, which shows the whole drawing at once.

        The speed changes right there in the program, so you can draw the slow parts slowly. When a picture is drawn with `pendown-cli`, it does nothing.
        """
        example title="Slow, then fast" """
        SETSPEED 3
        REPEAT 4 [FD 50 RT 90]
        SETSPEED 10
        REPEAT 36 [FD 20 RT 10]
        """
        see "speed" "wait"
      }
      speed {
        eg "SPEED"
        desc "outputs the turtle's speed, as a level from 1 to 11: whatever the speed slider shows, which both SETSPEED and moving the slider change. When a picture is drawn with pendown-cli, SPEED outputs 11. See SETSPEED."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        extension #true
        source "Python turtle.speed()"
        help """
        Outputs Kame's speed, as a level from 1 to 11, whatever the speed slider shows. See [SETSPEED].
        """
        example title="How fast?" """
        PRINT SPEED
        """
        see "setspeed"
      }
      setscrunch {
        eg "SETSCRUNCH xscale yscale"
        desc """
        adjusts the aspect ratio and scaling of the graphics display. After this command is used, all further turtle motion will be adjusted by multiplying the horizontal and vertical extent of the motion by the two numbers given as inputs. For example, after the instruction SETSCRUNCH 2 1 motion at a heading of 45 degrees will move twice as far horizontally as vertically. If your squares don’t come out square, try this. (Alternatively, you can deliberately misadjust the aspect ratio to draw an ellipse.)

        For all modern computers, both scale factors are initially 1. For DOS machines, the scale factors are initially set according to what the hardware claims the aspect ratio is, but the hardware sometimes lies. For DOS, the values set by SETSCRUNCH are remembered in a file (called scrunch.dat) and are automatically put into effect when a Logo session begins.
        """
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it corrected the shape of old screens, whose pixels weren't square."
      }
      refresh {
        eg "REFRESH"
        desc "(command) tells Logo to remember the turtle’s motions so that they can be used for high-resolution printing (wxWidgets) or to refresh the graphics window if it is moved, resized, or overlayed (non-wxWidgets). This is the default."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it saved memory by forgetting the drawing, and Pendown always keeps it."
      }
      norefresh {
        eg "NOREFRESH"
        desc "(command) tells Logo not to remember the turtle’s motions, which may be useful to save time and memory if your program is interactive or animated, rather than drawing a static picture you’ll want to print later (wxWidgets). In non-wxWidgets versions, using NOREFRESH may prevent Logo from restoring the graphics image after the window is moved, resized, or overlayed."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it saved memory by forgetting the drawing, and Pendown always keeps it."
      }
    }
    turtle-and-window-queries {
      shownp {
        eg """
        SHOWNP
        SHOWN?
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the turtle is shown (visible), FALSE if the turtle is hidden. See SHOWTURTLE and HIDETURTLE."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if Kame is shown, and `FALSE` if it's hidden.
        """
        example title="Is Kame there?" """
        PRINT SHOWNP
        HT
        PRINT SHOWNP
        """
        see "showturtle" "hideturtle"
      }
      screenmode {
        eg "SCREENMODE"
        desc "outputs the word TEXTSCREEN, SPLITSCREEN, or FULLSCREEN depending on the current screen mode."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it switched one screen between text and drawing, and Pendown always shows both."
      }
      turtlemode {
        eg "TURTLEMODE"
        desc "outputs the word WRAP, FENCE, or WINDOW depending on the current turtle mode."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `WRAP`, `WINDOW` or `FENCE`: what Kame does at the edge of the drawing area.
        """
        example title="Which mode?" """
        PRINT TURTLEMODE
        FENCE
        PRINT TURTLEMODE
        """
        see "wrap" "window" "fence"
      }
      labelsize {
        eg "LABELSIZE"
        desc "(wxWidgets only) outputs a list of two positive integers, the width and height of characters displayed by LABEL measured in turtle steps (which will be different from screen pixels if SETSCRUNCH has been used). There is no SETLABELSIZE because the width and height of a font are not separately controllable, so the inverse of this operation is SETLABELHEIGHT, which takes just one number for the desired height."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    pen-and-background-control {
      pendown {
        eg """
        PENDOWN
        PD
        """
        desc "sets the pen’s position to DOWN, without changing its mode."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Puts the pen down, so Kame draws as it moves. The pen is down to start with.
        """
        example title="Dots and dashes" """
        REPEAT 6 [PD FD 10 PU FD 10 PD FD 30 PU FD 10]
        """
        see "penup" "pendownp"
      }
      penup {
        eg """
        PENUP
        PU
        """
        desc "sets the pen’s position to UP, without changing its mode."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Lifts the pen, so Kame moves without drawing, until [PENDOWN]. Shapes are still filled while the pen is up, if a fill colour is set.
        """
        example title="Moving without drawing" """
        REPEAT 3 [CIRCLE 30 PU FD 100 PD]
        """
        see "pendown" "pendownp"
      }
      penpaint {
        eg """
        PENPAINT
        PPT
        """
        desc "sets the pen’s position to DOWN and mode to PAINT."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      penerase {
        eg """
        PENERASE
        PE
        """
        desc "sets the pen’s position to DOWN and mode to ERASE."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      penreverse {
        eg """
        PENREVERSE
        PX
        """
        desc "sets the pen’s position to DOWN and mode to REVERSE. (This may interact in system-dependent ways with use of color.)"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      setpencolor {
        eg """
        SETPENCOLOR colornumber.or.rgblist
        SETPC colornumber.or.rgblist
        """
        desc """
        sets the pen color to the given number, which must be a nonnegative integer. There are initial assignments for the first 16 colors:

             0  black        1  blue         2  green        3  cyan
             4  red          5  magenta      6  yellow       7 white
             8  brown        9  tan         10  forest      11  aqua
            12  salmon      13  purple      14  orange      15  grey

        but other colors can be assigned to numbers by the PALETTE command. Alternatively, sets the pen color to the given RGB values (a list of three nonnegative numbers less than 100 specifying the percent saturation of red, green, and blue in the desired color).
        """
        differs """
        A colour can be given in any of these forms, here and everywhere else a colour is accepted (SETBACKGROUND, SETFILLCOLOR, SETPALETTE, FILLED):

        - a palette number, as above;
        - an RGB list [r g b], with each value from 0 to 255, not a percentage;
        - an RGBA list [r g b a], where a is the opacity from 0 (invisible) to 255 (solid). Each move is drawn separately, so a see-through line is darker at the points where two moves meet;
        - one of the 148 CSS colour names, in any case, such as "red or "cornflowerblue;
        - a hex word: "#ff8800, the short form "#f80, or with opacity "#ff880080 or "#f808.

        A number is always a palette number, a list is always RGB or RGBA, a word starting with # is hex, and any other word is a colour name.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Sets the colour Kame draws with. A colour can be given in any of these forms, here and everywhere else a colour is wanted:
        - a palette number from 0 to 15: 0 black, 1 blue, 2 green, 3 cyan, 4 red, 5 magenta, 6 yellow, 7 white, 8 brown, 9 tan, 10 forest, 11 aqua, 12 salmon, 13 purple, 14 orange and 15 grey;
        - a colour name, such as `"red` or `"cornflowerblue`, from the 148 that web pages use;
        - a list of red, green and blue, each from 0 to 255, such as `[255 128 0]`;
        - the same with a fourth number for how solid it is, from 0 for invisible to 255 for solid, such as `[255 128 0 128]`;
        - a hex word, such as `"#ff8000`, `"#f80`, or `"#ff800080` with how solid it is.

        Every colour, with a picker to find one and copy it into your program, is on [Colours].

        Kame starts with 7, white.
        """
        example title="Rainbow" """
        SETPENSIZE 8
        MAKE "colours [red orange yellow green blue purple]
        REPEAT 6 [SETPC ITEM REPCOUNT :colours FD 30 * REPCOUNT RT 60]
        """
        example title="Every palette colour" """
        SETPENSIZE 12
        REPEAT 16 [SETPC REPCOUNT - 1 FD 120 BK 120 RT 22.5]
        """
        see "colours" "pencolor" "setbackground" "setfillcolor" "setpalette"
      }
      setpalette {
        eg "SETPALETTE colornumber rgblist"
        desc "sets the actual color corresponding to a given number, if allowed by the hardware and operating system. Colornumber must be an integer greater than or equal to 8. (Logo tries to keep the first 8 colors constant.) The second input is a list of three nonnegative numbers less than 100 specifying the percent saturation of red, green, and blue in the desired color."
        differs "The second input can be any of our colour forms except a palette number: see SETPENCOLOR. RGB values are from 0 to 255, not percentages. Colour numbers 8 to 15 can be changed, and Run puts them back."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Changes the colour a palette number stands for. The first input is a number from 8 to 15, and the second is any colour except a palette number: see [Colours]. Run puts the palette back as it was.
        """
        example title="My own colour 8" """
        SETPALETTE 8 [255 105 180]
        SETPC 8
        SETPENSIZE 6
        REPEAT 5 [FD 150 RT 144]
        """
        see "colours" "palette" "setpencolor"
      }
      setpensize {
        eg "SETPENSIZE size"
        desc "sets the thickness of the pen. The input is either a single positive integer or a list of two positive integers (for horizontal and vertical thickness). Some versions pay no attention to the second number, but always have a square pen."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Sets how thick Kame's lines are, in turtle steps. Kame starts with 1.
        """
        example title="Thicker and thicker" """
        RT 90
        REPEAT 5 [SETPENSIZE REPCOUNT * 3 PU SETXY -150 120 - REPCOUNT * 40 PD FD 300]
        """
        see "pensize"
      }
      setpenpattern {
        eg "SETPENPATTERN pattern"
        desc "sets hardware-dependent pen characteristics. This command is not guaranteed compatible between implementations on different machines."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it set pen patterns that depended on the computer, with no defined behaviour."
      }
      setpen {
        eg "SETPEN list  (library procedure)"
        desc "sets the pen’s position, mode, thickness, and hardware-dependent characteristics according to the information in the input list, which should be taken from an earlier invocation of PEN."
        see "pen"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      setbackground {
        eg """
        SETBACKGROUND colornumber.or.rgblist
        SETBG colornumber.or.rgblist
        """
        desc "set the screen background color by slot number or RGB values. See SETPENCOLOR for details."
        differs "The colour can be any of our colour forms: see SETPENCOLOR. The background is always solid, so any opacity is ignored."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Sets the colour behind the drawing, in any of the forms on [Colours]. What's already drawn stays. The background is always solid, so how solid the colour is doesn't matter. It starts as 0, black.
        """
        example title="Stars at night" """
        SETBG "midnightblue
        SETPC "yellow
        REPEAT 5 [FD 150 RT 144]
        """
        see "colours" "background" "setpencolor"
      }
      setfillcolor {
        eg """
        SETFILLCOLOR color
        SETFC color
        """
        desc """
        sets the colour that shapes (CIRCLE, ELLIPSE, SQUARE, RECTANGLE) are filled with. The colour can be any of the forms SETPENCOLOR accepts. SETFILLCOLOR "none turns filling off, and "none is the default, so shapes are drawn as outlines only. When a fill colour is set, shapes are filled even when the pen is up, so pen up with a fill colour draws a filled shape with no outline.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        extension #true
        source "LibreLogo (FILLCOLOR), FMSLogo (SETFLOODCOLOR)"
        help """
        Sets the colour that shapes, [CIRCLE], [ELLIPSE], [SQUARE] and [RECTANGLE], are filled with, in any of the forms on [Colours]. `SETFC "none` turns filling off, and that's how Kame starts, so shapes are only outlines.

        A shape is filled even when the pen is up, so with the pen up you get a filled shape with no outline.
        """
        example title="Filled and outlined" """
        SETFC "skyblue
        SETPC "navy
        SETPENSIZE 4
        CIRCLE 100
        """
        example title="See-through" """
        SETFC [255 0 0 100]
        PU
        REPEAT 3 [CIRCLE 80 FD 70 RT 120]
        """
        see "colours" "fillcolor" "setpencolor" "circle"
      }
    }
    pen-queries {
      pendownp {
        eg """
        PENDOWNP
        PENDOWN?
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the pen is down, FALSE if it’s up."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs `TRUE` if the pen is down, and `FALSE` if it's up.
        """
        example title="Is the pen down?" """
        PRINT PENDOWNP
        PU
        PRINT PENDOWNP
        """
        see "pendown" "penup"
      }
      penmode {
        eg "PENMODE"
        desc "outputs one of the words PAINT, ERASE, or REVERSE according to the current pen mode."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      pencolor {
        eg """
        PENCOLOR
        PC
        """
        desc """
        outputs a color number, a nonnegative integer that is associated with a particular color, or a list of RGB values if such a list was used as the most recent input to SETPENCOLOR. There are initial assignments for the first 16 colors:

             0  black        1  blue         2  green        3  cyan
             4  red          5  magenta      6  yellow       7 white
             8  brown        9  tan         10  forest      11  aqua
            12  salmon      13  purple      14  orange      15  grey

        but other colors can be assigned to numbers by the PALETTE command.
        """
        differs "Outputs the colour in whichever form it was set: a palette number, an RGB or RGBA list, a colour name or a hex word (see SETPENCOLOR)."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the colour Kame draws with, in the form it was set: a palette number, a list, a colour name or a hex word. See [Colours].
        """
        example title="Which colour?" """
        PRINT PENCOLOR
        SETPC "tomato
        PRINT PENCOLOR
        """
        see "colours" "setpencolor"
      }
      palette {
        eg "PALETTE colornumber"
        desc "outputs a list of three nonnegative numbers less than 100 specifying the percent saturation of red, green, and blue in the color associated with the given number."
        differs "Outputs an RGB list with values from 0 to 255, or an RGBA list if the colour isn't fully opaque."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the colour a palette number stands for, as a list of red, green and blue from 0 to 255, with a fourth number if it isn't solid. The palette's colours are on [Colours].
        """
        example title="What's in the palette" """
        SHOW PALETTE 4
        SHOW PALETTE 14
        """
        see "colours" "setpalette"
      }
      pensize {
        eg "PENSIZE"
        desc """
        outputs a list of two positive integers, specifying the horizontal and vertical thickness of the turtle pen. (In some implementations, including wxWidgets, the two numbers are always equal.)

            PENPATTERN

        outputs system-specific pen information.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs how thick Kame's lines are, as a list of two numbers, which are always the same.
        """
        example title="How thick?" """
        SETPENSIZE 5
        SHOW PENSIZE
        """
        see "setpensize"
      }
      pen {
        eg "PEN  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs a list containing the pen’s position, mode, thickness, and hardware-specific characteristics, for use by SETPEN."
        see "setpen"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      background {
        eg """
        BACKGROUND
        BG
        """
        desc "outputs the graphics background color, either as a slot number or as an RGB list, whichever way it was set. (See PENCOLOR.)"
        differs "Outputs the colour in whichever form it was set: a palette number, an RGB or RGBA list, a colour name or a hex word (see SETPENCOLOR)."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the background colour, in the form it was set. See [Colours].
        """
        example title="What's behind?" """
        SETBG [20 40 80]
        SHOW BACKGROUND
        """
        see "colours" "setbackground"
      }
      fillcolor {
        eg """
        FILLCOLOR
        FC
        """
        desc "outputs the fill colour used by shapes, in whichever form it was set (see SETPENCOLOR), or the word NONE if filling is off."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        extension #true
        source "LibreLogo, FMSLogo (FLOODCOLOR)"
        help """
        Outputs the colour shapes are filled with, in the form it was set, or `none` if filling is off. See [Colours].
        """
        example title="Which fill?" """
        PRINT FILLCOLOR
        SETFC "gold
        PRINT FILLCOLOR
        """
        see "colours" "setfillcolor"
      }
    }
    saving-and-loading-pictures {
      savepict {
        eg "SAVEPICT filename"
        desc "command. Writes a file with the specified name containing the state of the graphics window, including any nonstandard color palette settings, in Logo’s internal format. This picture can be restored to the screen using LOADPICT. The format is not portable between platforms, nor is it readable by other programs. EPSPICT to export Logo graphics for other programs."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it uses UCBLogo's own picture format. Use Export PNG, and keep the program to draw it again."
      }
      loadpict {
        eg "LOADPICT filename"
        desc "command. Reads the specified file, which must have been written by a SAVEPICT command, and restores the graphics window and color palette settings to the values stored in the file. Any drawing previously on the screen is cleared."
        see "savepict"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it uses UCBLogo's own picture format. Use Export PNG, and keep the program to draw it again."
      }
      epspict {
        eg "EPSPICT filename"
        desc "command. Writes a file with the specified name, containing an Encapsulated Postscript (EPS) representation of the state of the graphics window. This file can be imported into other programs that understand EPS format. Restrictions: the drawing cannot use FILL, PENERASE, or PENREVERSE; any such instructions will be ignored in the translation to Postscript form."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it saves PostScript. Use Export PNG."
      }
    }
    mouse-queries {
      mousepos {
        eg "MOUSEPOS"
        desc "outputs the coordinates of the mouse, provided that it’s within the graphics window, in turtle coordinates. If the mouse is outside the graphics window, then the last position within the window is returned. Exception: If a mouse button is pressed within the graphics window and held while the mouse is dragged outside the window, the mouse’s position is returned as if the window were big enough to include it."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      clickpos {
        eg "CLICKPOS"
        desc "outputs the coordinates that the mouse was at when a mouse button was most recently pushed, provided that that position was within the graphics window, in turtle coordinates. (wxWidgets only)"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      buttonp {
        eg """
        BUTTONP
        BUTTON?
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if a mouse button is down and the mouse is over the graphics window. Once the button is down, BUTTONP remains true until the button is released, even if the mouse is dragged out of the graphics window."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
      button {
        eg "BUTTON"
        desc "outputs 0 if no mouse button has been pushed inside the Logo window since the last call to BUTTON. Otherwise, it outputs an integer between 1 and 3 indicating which button was most recently pressed. Ordinarily 1 means left, 2 means right, and 3 means center, but operating systems may reconfigure these."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 6
      }
    }
  }
  workspace-management {
    procedure-definition {
      to {
        eg "TO procname :input1 :input2 ...  (special form)"
        names "END"
        desc """
        command. Prepares Logo to accept a procedure definition. The procedure will be named procname and there must not already be a procedure by that name. The inputs will be called input1 etc. Any number of inputs are allowed, including none. Names of procedures and inputs are case-insensitive.

        Unlike every other Logo procedure, TO takes as its inputs the actual words typed in the instruction line, as if they were all quoted, rather than the results of evaluating expressions to provide the inputs. (That’s what special form means.)

        This version of Logo allows variable numbers of inputs to a procedure. After the procedure name come four kinds of things, in this order:

                1.   0 or more REQUIRED inputs    :FOO :FROBOZZ
                2.   0 or more OPTIONAL inputs    [:BAZ 87] [:THINGO 5+9]
                3.   0 or 1 REST input            [:GARPLY]
                4.   0 or 1 DEFAULT number        5

        Every procedure has a minimum, default, and maximum number of inputs. (The latter can be infinite.)

        The minimum number of inputs is the number of required inputs, which must come first. A required input is indicated by the

            :inputname

        notation.

        After all the required inputs can be zero or more optional inputs, each of which is represented by the following notation:

            [:inputname default.value.expression]

        When the procedure is invoked, if actual inputs are not supplied for these optional inputs, the default value expressions are evaluated to set values for the corresponding input names. The inputs are processed from left to right, so a default value expression can be based on earlier inputs. Example:

            to proc :inlist [:startvalue first :inlist]

        If the procedure is invoked by saying

            proc [a b c]

        then the variable inlist will have the value [A B C] and the variable startvalue will have the value A. If the procedure is invoked by saying

            (proc [a b c] "x)

        then inlist will have the value [A B C] and startvalue will have the value X.

        After all the required and optional input can come a single rest input, represented by the following notation:

            [:inputname]

        This is a rest input rather than an optional input because there is no default value expression. There can be at most one rest input. When the procedure is invoked, the value of this inputname will be a list containing all of the actual inputs provided that were not used for required or optional inputs. Example:

            to proc :in1 [:in2 "foo] [:in3 "baz] [:in4]

        If this procedure is invoked by saying

            proc "x

        then in1 has the value X, in2 has the value FOO, in3 has the value BAZ, and in4 has the value [] (the empty list). If it’s invoked by saying

            (proc "a "b "c "d "e)

        then in1 has the value A, in2 has the value B, in3 has the value C, and in4 has the value [D E].

        The maximum number of inputs for a procedure is infinite if a rest input is given; otherwise, it is the number of required inputs plus the number of optional inputs.

        The default number of inputs for a procedure, which is the number of inputs that it will accept if its invocation is not enclosed in parentheses, is ordinarily equal to the minimum number. If you want a different default number you can indicate that by putting the desired default number as the last thing on the TO line. example:

            to proc :in1 [:in2 "foo] [:in3] 3

        This procedure has a minimum of one input, a default of three inputs, and an infinite maximum.

        Logo responds to the TO command by entering procedure definition mode. The prompt character changes from ? to > and whatever instructions you type become part of the definition until you type a line containing only the word END.
        """
        differs "The whole definition can also go on one line: TO SQUARE :n REPEAT 4 [FD :n RT 90] END. In UCBLogo, END must be on a line of its own. TO must still start a line, and the title (the name and inputs) must be on the same line as TO."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Teaches Kame a new word: a procedure. `TO` starts it, with the procedure's name, and any inputs after it as `:names`. The instructions come next, and `END` finishes it. Then you can use the new word like any other command.

        The whole definition can go on one line, as in `TO SQUARE.ONE :n REPEAT 4 [FD :n RT 90] END`. A procedure can call other procedures, and itself, which is recursion. A procedure that [OUTPUT]s a value can be used as an input to other commands.
        """
        example title="A new word" """
        TO TRIANGLE :size
          REPEAT 3 [FD :size RT 120]
        END
        TRIANGLE 100
        RT 180
        TRIANGLE 150
        """
        example title="A procedure that calls itself" """
        TO TREE :size
          IF :size < 10 [STOP]
          FD :size
          LT 30 TREE :size * 0.7
          RT 60 TREE :size * 0.7
          LT 30 BK :size
        END
        TREE 120
        """
        example title="A procedure that outputs" """
        TO DOUBLE :n
          OUTPUT :n * 2
        END
        PRINT DOUBLE 21
        """
        see "output" "stop" "local"
      }
      define {
        eg "DEFINE procname text"
        desc "command. Defines a procedure with name procname and text text. If there is already a procedure with the same name, the new definition replaces the old one. The text input must be a list whose members are lists. The first member is a list of inputs; it looks like a TO line but without the word TO, without the procedure name, and without the colons before input names. In other words, the members of this first sublist are words for the names of required inputs and lists for the names of optional or rest inputs. The remaining sublists of the text input make up the body of the procedure, with one sublist for each instruction line of the body. (There is no END line in the text input.) It is an error to redefine a primitive procedure unless the variable REDEFP has the value TRUE."
        see "redefp"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      text {
        eg "TEXT procname"
        desc "outputs the text of the procedure named procname in the form expected by DEFINE: a list of lists, the first of which describes the inputs to the procedure and the rest of which are the lines of its body. The text does not reflect formatting information used when the procedure was defined, such as continuation lines and extra spaces."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      fulltext {
        eg "FULLTEXT procname"
        desc "outputs a representation of the procedure procname in which formatting information is preserved. If the procedure was defined with TO, EDIT, or LOAD, then the output is a list of words. Each word represents one entire line of the definition in the form output by READWORD, including extra spaces and continuation lines. The last member of the output represents the END line. If the procedure was defined with DEFINE, then the output is a list of lists. If these lists are printed, one per line, the result will look like a definition using TO. Note: the output from FULLTEXT is not suitable for use as input to DEFINE!"
        see "text"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it gives a procedure's text for an editor, and Pendown's editor already shows your program."
      }
      copydef {
        eg "COPYDEF newname oldname"
        desc """
        command. Makes newname a procedure identical to oldname. The latter may be a primitive. If newname was already defined, its previous definition is lost. If newname was already a primitive, the redefinition is not permitted unless the variable REDEFP has the value TRUE.

        Note: dialects of Logo differ as to the order of inputs to COPYDEF. This dialect uses "MAKE order," not "NAME order."
        """
        see "redefp" "save" "po" "pot"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    variable-definition {
      make {
        eg "MAKE varname value"
        desc "command. Assigns the value value to the variable named varname, which must be a word. Variable names are case-insensitive. If a variable with the same name already exists, the value of that variable is changed. If not, a new global variable is created."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Gives a variable a value. The first input is the variable's name, quoted: `MAKE "size 100`. Then `:size` gives the value back.

        If there's already a variable with that name, its value changes. Otherwise a new one is made, which every procedure can see. Inside a procedure, use [LOCALMAKE] for a variable only that procedure uses.
        """
        example title="Remembering a size" """
        MAKE "size 120
        REPEAT 4 [FD :size RT 90]
        MAKE "size :size / 2
        REPEAT 4 [FD :size RT 90]
        """
        see "localmake" "thing" "name"
      }
      name {
        eg "NAME value varname  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Same as MAKE but with the inputs in reverse order."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Gives a variable a value, like [MAKE], but with its inputs the other way round: the value first, then the name.
        """
        example title="The other way round" """
        NAME 42 "answer
        PRINT :answer
        """
        see "make"
      }
      local {
        eg """
        LOCAL varname
        LOCAL varnamelist
        (LOCAL varname1 varname2 ...)
        """
        desc "command. Accepts as inputs one or more words, or a list of words. A variable is created for each of these words, with that word as its name. The variables are local to the currently running procedure. Logo variables follow dynamic scope rules; a variable that is local to a procedure is available to any subprocedure invoked by that procedure. The variables created by LOCAL have no initial value; they must be assigned a value (e.g., with MAKE) before the procedure attempts to read their value."
        differs "At the top level, outside any procedure, LOCAL is an error."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Makes variables that belong to the procedure it's in, so they don't change variables of the same name elsewhere. Give a quoted name, a list of names, or several names in parentheses. They have no value until you [MAKE] one.

        A procedure's local variables can be seen by the procedures it calls. Outside any procedure, `LOCAL` is an error.
        """
        example title="Keeping a variable to itself" """
        MAKE "n 1
        TO COUNTDOWN
          LOCAL "n
          MAKE "n 3
          REPEAT 3 [PRINT :n MAKE "n :n - 1]
        END
        COUNTDOWN
        PRINT :n
        """
        see "localmake" "make"
      }
      localmake {
        eg "LOCALMAKE varname value  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Makes the named variable local, like LOCAL, and assigns it the given value, like MAKE."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Makes a variable that belongs to the procedure it's in, like [LOCAL], and gives it a value, like [MAKE].
        """
        example title="A local size" """
        TO SPIRAL :turns
          LOCALMAKE "length 5
          REPEAT :turns [FD :length RT 90 MAKE "length :length + 5]
        END
        SPIRAL 40
        """
        see "local" "make"
      }
      thing {
        eg """
        THING varname
        :quoted.varname
        """
        desc """
        outputs the value of the variable whose name is the input. If there is more than one such variable, the innermost local variable of that name is chosen. The colon notation is an abbreviation not for THING but for the combination

            thing "

        so that :FOO means THING "FOO.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs the value of the variable with this name. `:size` is short for `THING "size`, but `THING` can take a name worked out while the program runs.
        """
        example title="A name worked out" """
        MAKE "side1 50
        MAKE "side2 150
        PRINT THING "side1
        PRINT THING WORD "side 2
        """
        see "make"
      }
      global {
        eg """
        GLOBAL varname
        GLOBAL varnamelist
        (GLOBAL varname1 varname2 ...)
        """
        desc "command. Accepts as inputs one or more words, or a list of words. A global variable is created for each of these words, with that word as its name. The only reason this is necessary is that you might want to use the \"setter\" notation SETXYZ for a variable XYZ that does not already have a value; GLOBAL \"XYZ makes that legal. Note: If there is currently a local variable of the same name, this command does *not* make Logo use the global value instead of the local one."
        differs "Pendown has no setter notation (SETXYZ), and MAKE creates global variables anyway, so GLOBAL only checks its inputs."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Makes sure there are global variables with these names, with no value until you [MAKE] one. Give a quoted name, a list of names, or several names in parentheses.

        You rarely need it in Pendown: [MAKE] makes a global variable anyway when there isn't one.
        """
        example title="Declaring a global" """
        GLOBAL "score
        MAKE "score 10
        PRINT :score
        """
        see "make" "local"
      }
    }
    property-lists {
      pprop {
        eg "PPROP plistname propname value"
        desc "command. Adds a property to the plistname property list with name propname and value value."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      gprop {
        eg "GPROP plistname propname"
        desc "outputs the value of the propname property in the plistname property list, or the empty list if there is no such property."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      remprop {
        eg "REMPROP plistname propname"
        desc "command. Removes the property named propname from the property list named plistname."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      plist {
        eg "PLIST plistname"
        desc "outputs a list whose odd-numbered members are the names, and whose even-numbered members are the values, of the properties in the property list named plistname. The output is a copy of the actual property list; changing properties later will not magically change a list output earlier by PLIST."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    workspace-predicates {
      procedurep {
        eg """
        PROCEDUREP name
        PROCEDURE? name
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is the name of a procedure."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      primitivep {
        eg """
        PRIMITIVEP name
        PRIMITIVE? name
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is the name of a primitive procedure (one built into Logo). Note that some of the procedures described in this document are library procedures, not primitives."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      definedp {
        eg """
        DEFINEDP name
        DEFINED? name
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is the name of a user-defined procedure, including a library procedure."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      namep {
        eg """
        NAMEP name
        NAME? name
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is the name of a variable."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      plistp {
        eg """
        PLISTP name
        PLIST? name
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the input is the name of a nonempty property list. (In principle every word is the name of a property list; if you haven’t put any properties in it, PLIST of that name outputs an empty list, rather than giving an error message.)"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
    workspace-queries {
      contents {
        eg "CONTENTS"
        desc "outputs a \"contents list,\" i.e., a list of three lists containing names of defined procedures, variables, and property lists respectively. This list includes all unburied named items in the workspace."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists the workspace for commands Pendown doesn't have. The variables panel shows your variables, and the editor your procedures."
      }
      buried {
        eg "BURIED"
        desc "outputs a contents list including all buried named items in the workspace."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists the workspace for commands Pendown doesn't have. The variables panel shows your variables, and the editor your procedures."
      }
      traced {
        eg "TRACED"
        desc "outputs a contents list including all traced named items in the workspace."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists the workspace for commands Pendown doesn't have. The variables panel shows your variables, and the editor your procedures."
      }
      stepped {
        eg "STEPPED"
        desc "outputs a contents list including all stepped named items in the workspace."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists the workspace for commands Pendown doesn't have. The variables panel shows your variables, and the editor your procedures."
      }
      procedures {
        eg "PROCEDURES"
        desc "outputs a list of the names of all unburied user-defined procedures in the workspace. Note that this is a list of names, not a contents list. (However, procedures that require a contents list as input will accept this list.)"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists the workspace for commands Pendown doesn't have. The variables panel shows your variables, and the editor your procedures."
      }
      primitives {
        eg "PRIMITIVES"
        desc "outputs a list of the names of all primitive procedures in the workspace. Note that this is a list of names, not a contents list. (However, procedures that require a contents list as input will accept this list.)"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists every built-in command, and the help panel shows them all."
      }
      names {
        eg "NAMES"
        desc "outputs a contents list consisting of an empty list (indicating no procedure names) followed by a list of all unburied variable names in the workspace."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists the workspace for commands Pendown doesn't have. The variables panel shows your variables, and the editor your procedures."
      }
      plists {
        eg "PLISTS"
        desc "outputs a contents list consisting of two empty lists (indicating no procedures or variables) followed by a list of all unburied nonempty property lists in the workspace."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists the workspace for commands Pendown doesn't have. The variables panel shows your variables, and the editor your procedures."
      }
      namelist {
        eg """
        NAMELIST varname  (library procedure)
        NAMELIST varnamelist
        """
        desc "outputs a contents list consisting of an empty list followed by a list of the name or names given as input. This is useful in conjunction with workspace control procedures that require a contents list as input."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists the workspace for commands Pendown doesn't have. The variables panel shows your variables, and the editor your procedures."
      }
      pllist {
        eg """
        PLLIST plname  (library procedure)
        PLLIST plnamelist
        """
        desc "outputs a contents list consisting of two empty lists followed by a list of the name or names given as input. This is useful in conjunction with workspace control procedures that require a contents list as input."
        see "contents"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it lists the workspace for commands Pendown doesn't have. The variables panel shows your variables, and the editor your procedures."
      }
      arity {
        eg "ARITY procedurename"
        desc "outputs a list of three numbers: the minimum, default, and maximum number of inputs for the procedure whose name is the input. It is an error if there is no such procedure. A maximum of -1 means that the number of inputs is unlimited."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      nodes {
        eg "NODES"
        desc "outputs a list of two numbers. The first represents the number of nodes of memory currently in use. The second shows the maximum number of nodes that have been in use at any time since the last invocation of NODES. (A node is a small block of computer memory as used by Logo. Each number uses one node. Each non-numeric word uses one node, plus some non-node memory for the characters in the word. Each array takes one node, plus some non-node memory, as well as the memory required by its elements. Each list requires one node per element, as well as the memory within the elements.) If you want to track the memory use of an algorithm, it is best if you invoke GC at the beginning of each iteration, since otherwise the maximum will include storage that is unused but not yet collected."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it managed memory by hand, which Pendown does for you."
      }
    }
    workspace-inspection {
      po {
        eg """
        PRINTOUT contentslist
        PO contentslist
        """
        desc "command. Prints to the write stream the definitions of all procedures, variables, and property lists named in the input contents list."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints definitions because there was no editor, and Pendown's editor shows them."
      }
      poall {
        eg "POALL  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Prints all unburied definitions in the workspace. Abbreviates PO CONTENTS."
        see "contents"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints definitions because there was no editor, and Pendown's editor shows them."
      }
      pops {
        eg "POPS  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Prints the definitions of all unburied procedures in the workspace. Abbreviates PO PROCEDURES."
        see "po" "procedures"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints definitions because there was no editor, and Pendown's editor shows them."
      }
      pons {
        eg "PONS  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Prints the definitions of all unburied variables in the workspace. Abbreviates PO NAMES."
        see "po" "names"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints definitions because there was no editor, and Pendown's editor shows them."
      }
      popls {
        eg "POPLS  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Prints the contents of all unburied nonempty property lists in the workspace. Abbreviates PO PLISTS."
        see "po" "plists"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints definitions because there was no editor, and Pendown's editor shows them."
      }
      pon {
        eg """
        PON varname  (library procedure)
        PON varnamelist
        """
        desc """
        command. Prints the definitions of the named variable(s).

        Abbreviates PO NAMELIST varname(list).
        """
        see "po" "namelist"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints definitions because there was no editor, and Pendown's editor shows them."
      }
      popl {
        eg """
        POPL plname  (library procedure)
        POPL plnamelist
        """
        desc """
        command. Prints the definitions of the named property list(s).

        Abbreviates PO PLLIST plname(list).
        """
        see "po" "pllist"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints definitions because there was no editor, and Pendown's editor shows them."
      }
      pot {
        eg "POT contentslist"
        desc "command. Prints the title lines of the named procedures and the definitions of the named variables and property lists. For property lists, the entire list is shown on one line instead of as a series of PPROP instructions as in PO."
        see "pprop" "po"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints definitions because there was no editor, and Pendown's editor shows them."
      }
      pots {
        eg "POTS  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Prints the title lines of all unburied procedures in the workspace. Abbreviates POT PROCEDURES."
        see "procedures"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints definitions because there was no editor, and Pendown's editor shows them."
      }
    }
    workspace-control {
      erase {
        eg """
        ERASE contentslist
        ER contentslist
        """
        desc "command. Erases from the workspace the procedures, variables, and property lists named in the input. Primitive procedures may not be erased unless the variable REDEFP has the value TRUE."
        see "redefp"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "your program is what's in the editor, so you remove a procedure by deleting it there."
      }
      erall {
        eg "ERALL"
        desc "command. Erases all unburied procedures, variables, and property lists from the workspace. Abbreviates ERASE CONTENTS."
        see "contents"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "your program is what's in the editor, so you remove a procedure by deleting it there."
      }
      erps {
        eg "ERPS"
        desc """
        command. Erases all unburied procedures from the workspace.

        Abbreviates ERASE PROCEDURES.
        """
        see "erase" "procedures"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "your program is what's in the editor, so you remove a procedure by deleting it there."
      }
      erns {
        eg "ERNS"
        desc "command. Erases all unburied variables from the workspace. Abbreviates ERASE NAMES."
        see "erase" "names"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "your program is what's in the editor, so you remove a procedure by deleting it there."
      }
      erpls {
        eg "ERPLS"
        desc """
        command. Erases all unburied property lists from the workspace.

        Abbreviates ERASE PLISTS.
        """
        see "erase" "plists"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "your program is what's in the editor, so you remove a procedure by deleting it there."
      }
      ern {
        eg """
        ERN varname  (library procedure)
        ERN varnamelist
        """
        desc "command. Erases from the workspace the variable(s) named in the input. Abbreviates ERASE NAMELIST varname(list)."
        see "erase" "namelist"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "your program is what's in the editor, so you remove a procedure by deleting it there."
      }
      erpl {
        eg """
        ERPL plname  (library procedure)
        ERPL plnamelist
        """
        desc "command. Erases from the workspace the property list(s) named in the input. Abbreviates ERASE PLLIST plname(list)."
        see "erase" "pllist"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "your program is what's in the editor, so you remove a procedure by deleting it there."
      }
      bury {
        eg "BURY contentslist"
        desc "command. Buries the procedures, variables, and property lists named in the input. A buried item is not included in the lists output by CONTENTS, PROCEDURES, VARIABLES, and PLISTS, but is included in the list output by BURIED. By implication, buried things are not printed by POALL or saved by SAVE."
        see "contents" "procedures" "pons" "plists" "poall" "save"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it hides procedures from commands Pendown doesn't have."
      }
      buryall {
        eg "BURYALL  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Abbreviates BURY CONTENTS."
        see "contents"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it hides procedures from commands Pendown doesn't have."
      }
      buryname {
        eg """
        BURYNAME varname  (library procedure)
        BURYNAME varnamelist
        """
        desc "command. Abbreviates BURY NAMELIST varname(list)."
        see "bury" "namelist"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it hides procedures from commands Pendown doesn't have."
      }
      unbury {
        eg "UNBURY contentslist"
        desc "command. Unburies the procedures, variables, and property lists named in the input. That is, the named items will be returned to view in CONTENTS, etc."
        see "contents"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it hides procedures from commands Pendown doesn't have."
      }
      unburyall {
        eg "UNBURYALL  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Abbreviates UNBURY BURIED."
        see "buried"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it hides procedures from commands Pendown doesn't have."
      }
      unburyname {
        eg """
        UNBURYNAME varname  (library procedure)
        UNBURYNAME varnamelist
        """
        desc "command. Abbreviates UNBURY NAMELIST varname(list)."
        see "unbury" "namelist"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it hides procedures from commands Pendown doesn't have."
      }
      buriedp {
        eg """
        BURIEDP contentslist
        BURIED? contentslist
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the first procedure, variable, or property list named in the contents list is buried, FALSE if not. Only the first thing in the list is tested; the most common use will be with a word as input, naming a procedure, but a contents list is allowed so that you can BURIEDP [[] [variable]] or BURIEDP [[] [] [proplist]]."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it hides procedures from commands Pendown doesn't have."
      }
      trace {
        eg "TRACE contentslist"
        desc "command. Marks the named items for tracing. A message is printed whenever a traced procedure is invoked, giving the actual input values, and whenever a traced procedure STOPs or OUTPUTs. A message is printed whenever a new value is assigned to a traced variable using MAKE. A message is printed whenever a new property is given to a traced property list using PPROP."
        see "stop" "output" "make" "pprop"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it turns on tracing or stepping, and Pendown's Pause and Step line buttons do that."
      }
      untrace {
        eg "UNTRACE contentslist"
        desc "command. Turns off tracing for the named items."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it turns on tracing or stepping, and Pendown's Pause and Step line buttons do that."
      }
      tracedp {
        eg """
        TRACEDP contentslist
        TRACED? contentslist
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the first procedure, variable, or property list named in the contents list is traced, FALSE if not. Only the first thing in the list is tested; the most common use will be with a word as input, naming a procedure, but a contents list is allowed so that you can TRACEDP [[] [variable]] or TRACEDP [[] [] [proplist]]."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it turns on tracing or stepping, and Pendown's Pause and Step line buttons do that."
      }
      step {
        eg "STEP contentslist"
        desc "command. Marks the named items for stepping. Whenever a stepped procedure is invoked, each instruction line in the procedure body is printed before being executed, and Logo waits for the user to type a newline at the terminal. A message is printed whenever a stepped variable name is shadowed because a local variable of the same name is created either as a procedure input or by the LOCAL command."
        see "local"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it turns on tracing or stepping, and Pendown's Pause and Step line buttons do that."
      }
      unstep {
        eg "UNSTEP contentslist"
        desc "command. Turns off stepping for the named items."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it turns on tracing or stepping, and Pendown's Pause and Step line buttons do that."
      }
      steppedp {
        eg """
        STEPPEDP contentslist
        STEPPED? contentslist
        """
        desc "outputs TRUE if the first procedure, variable, or property list named in the contents list is stepped, FALSE if not. Only the first thing in the list is tested; the most common use will be with a word as input, naming a procedure, but a contents list is allowed so that you can STEPPEDP [[] [variable]] or STEPPEDP [[] [] [proplist]]."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it turns on tracing or stepping, and Pendown's Pause and Step line buttons do that."
      }
      edit {
        eg """
        EDIT contentslist
        ED contentslist
        (EDIT)
        (ED)
        """
        desc """
        command. If invoked with an input, EDIT writes the definitions of the named items into a temporary file and edits that file, using an editor that depends on the platform you’re using. In wxWidgets, and in the MacOS Classic version, there is an editor built into Logo. In the non-wxWidgets versions for Unix, MacOS X, Windows, and DOS, Logo uses your favorite editor as determined by the EDITOR environment variable. If you don’t have an EDITOR variable, edits the definitions using jove. If invoked without an input, EDIT edits the same file left over from a previous EDIT or EDITFILE instruction. When you leave the editor, Logo reads the revised definitions and modifies the workspace accordingly. It is not an error if the input includes names for which there is no previous definition.

        If there is a variable LOADNOISILY whose value is TRUE, then, after leaving the editor, TO commands in the temporary file print ‘procname defined’ (where procname is the name of the procedure being defined); if LOADNOISILY is FALSE or undefined, TO commands in the file are carried out silently.

        If there is an environment variable called TEMP, then Logo uses its value as the directory in which to write the temporary file used for editing.

        Exceptionally, the EDIT command can be used without its default input and without parentheses provided that nothing follows it on the instruction line.
        """
        see "loadnoisily" "editfile"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it opens an external text editor, and Pendown has its own."
      }
      editfile {
        eg "EDITFILE filename"
        desc """
        command. Starts the Logo editor, like EDIT, but instead of editing a temporary file it edits the file specified by the input. When you leave the editor, Logo reads the revised file, as for EDIT. EDITFILE also remembers the filename, so that a subsequent EDIT command with no input will re-edit the same file.

        EDITFILE is intended as an alternative to LOAD and SAVE. You can maintain a workspace file yourself, controlling the order in which definitions appear, maintaining comments in the file, and so on.

        In the wxWidgets version, EDITFILE asks whether or not you want to load the file into Logo when you finish editing. This allows you to use EDITFILE to edit data files without leaving Logo.
        """
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it opens an external text editor, and Pendown has its own."
      }
      edall {
        eg "EDALL  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Abbreviates EDIT CONTENTS."
        see "contents"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it opens an external text editor, and Pendown has its own."
      }
      edps {
        eg "EDPS  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Abbreviates EDIT PROCEDURES."
        see "edit" "procedures"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it opens an external text editor, and Pendown has its own."
      }
      edns {
        eg "EDNS  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Abbreviates EDIT NAMES."
        see "edit" "names"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it opens an external text editor, and Pendown has its own."
      }
      edpls {
        eg "EDPLS  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Abbreviates EDIT PLISTS."
        see "edit" "plists"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it opens an external text editor, and Pendown has its own."
      }
      edn {
        eg """
        EDN varname  (library procedure)
        EDN varnamelist
        """
        desc "command. Abbreviates EDIT NAMELIST varname(list)."
        see "edit" "namelist"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it opens an external text editor, and Pendown has its own."
      }
      edpl {
        eg """
        EDPL plname  (library procedure)
        EDPL plnamelist
        """
        desc "command. Abbreviates EDIT PLLIST plname(list)."
        see "edit" "pllist"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it opens an external text editor, and Pendown has its own."
      }
      save {
        eg "SAVE filename"
        desc """
        command. Saves the definitions of all unburied procedures, variables, and nonempty property lists in the named file. Equivalent to

            to save :filename
            local "oldwriter
            make "oldwriter writer
            openwrite :filename
            setwrite :filename
            poall
            setwrite :oldwriter
            close :filename
            end

        Exceptionally, SAVE can be used with no input and without parentheses if it is the last thing on the command line. In this case, the filename from the most recent LOAD or SAVE command will be used. (It is an error if there has been no previous LOAD or SAVE.)
        """
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it saves or loads a whole workspace. Use Open and Save for program files."
      }
      savel {
        eg "SAVEL contentslist filename  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Saves the definitions of the procedures, variables, and property lists specified by contentslist to the file named filename."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it saves or loads a whole workspace. Use Open and Save for program files."
      }
      load {
        eg "LOAD filename"
        desc "command. Reads instructions from the named file and executes them. The file can include procedure definitions with TO, and these are accepted even if a procedure by the same name already exists. If the file assigns a list value to a variable named STARTUP, then that list is run as an instructionlist after the file is loaded. If there is a variable LOADNOISILY whose value is TRUE, then TO commands in the file print ‘procname defined’ (where procname is the name of the procedure being defined); if LOADNOISILY is FALSE or undefined, TO commands in the file are carried out silently."
        see "startup" "loadnoisily"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it saves or loads a whole workspace. Use Open and Save for program files."
      }
      cslsload {
        eg "CSLSLOAD name"
        desc "command. Loads the named file, like LOAD, but from the directory containing the Computer Science Logo Style programs instead of the current user’s directory."
        see "load"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it loads programs from the Computer Science Logo Style books."
      }
      help {
        eg """
        HELP name
        (HELP)
        """
        desc """
        command. Prints information from the reference manual about the primitive procedure named by the input. With no input, lists all the primitives about which help is available. If there is an environment variable LOGOHELP, then its value is taken as the directory in which to look for help files, instead of the default help directory.

        If HELP is called with the name of a defined procedure for which there is no help file, it will print the title line of the procedure followed by lines from the procedure body that start with semicolon, stopping when a non-semicolon line is seen.

        Exceptionally, the HELP command can be used without its default input and without parentheses provided that nothing follows it on the instruction line.
        """
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it prints the manual, and Pendown's help panel shows it: press F1."
      }
      seteditor {
        eg "SETEDITOR path"
        desc "command. Tells Logo to use the specified program as its editor instead of the default editor. The format of a path depends on your operating system."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it sets where UCBLogo keeps its own files."
      }
      setlibloc {
        eg "SETLIBLOC path"
        desc "command. Tells Logo to use the specified directory as its library instead of the default. (Note that many Logo \"primitive\" procedures are actually found in the library, so they may become unavailable if your new library does not include them!) The format of a path depends on your operating system."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it sets where UCBLogo keeps its own files."
      }
      setcslsloc {
        eg "SETCSLSLOC path"
        desc "command. Tells Logo to use the specified directory for the CSLSLOAD command, instead of the default directory. The format of a path depends on your operating system."
        see "cslsload"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it sets where UCBLogo keeps its own files."
      }
      sethelploc {
        eg "SETHELPLOC path"
        desc "command. Tells Logo to look in the specified directory for the information provided by the HELP command, instead of the default directory. The format of a path depends on your operating system."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it sets where UCBLogo keeps its own files."
      }
      settemploc {
        eg "SETTEMPLOC path"
        desc "command. Tells Logo to write editor temporary files in the specified directory rather than in the default directory. You must have write permission for this directory. The format of a path depends on your operating system."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it sets where UCBLogo keeps its own files."
      }
      gc {
        eg """
        GC
        (GC anything)
        """
        desc "command. Runs the garbage collector, reclaiming unused nodes. Logo does this when necessary anyway, but you may want to use this command to control exactly when Logo does it. In particular, the numbers output by the NODES operation will not be very meaningful unless garbage has been collected. Another reason to use GC is that a garbage collection takes a noticeable fraction of a second, and you may want to schedule collections for times before or after some time-critical animation. If invoked with an argument (of any value), GC runs a full garbage collection, including GCTWA (Garbage Collect Truly Worthless Atoms, which means that it removes from Logo’s memory words that used to be procedure or variable names but aren’t any more); without an argument, GC does a generational garbage collection, which means that only recently created nodes are examined. (The latter is usually good enough.)"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it managed memory by hand, which Pendown does for you."
      }
      .setsegmentsize {
        eg ".SETSEGMENTSIZE num"
        desc "command. Sets the number of nodes that Logo allocates from the operating system at once to num, which must be a positive integer. The name is dotted because bad things will happen if you use a number that’s too small or too large for your computer. The initial value is 16,000 for most systems, but is smaller for 68000-based Macs. Making it larger will speed up computations (by reducing the number of garbage collections) at the cost of allocating more memory than necessary."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it managed memory by hand, which Pendown does for you."
      }
    }
  }
  control-structures {
    control {
      run {
        eg "RUN instructionlist"
        desc "command or operation. Runs the Logo instructions in the input list; outputs if the list contains an expression that outputs."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Runs a list as instructions. If the list works out a value, `RUN` outputs it. It lets a program build instructions and then run them.
        """
        example title="Instructions in a list" """
        MAKE "steps [FD 100 RT 120]
        REPEAT 3 [RUN :steps]
        """
        example title="A value from a list" """
        PRINT RUN [3 * 4]
        """
        see "runresult" "repeat"
      }
      runresult {
        eg "RUNRESULT instructionlist"
        desc """
        runs the instructions in the input; outputs an empty list if those instructions produce no output, or a list whose only member is the output from running the input instructionlist. Useful for inventing command-or-operation control structures:

            local "result
            make "result runresult [something]
            if emptyp :result [stop]
            output first :result
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Runs a list as instructions, like [RUN], and outputs a list: empty if the instructions gave no value, or holding the one value they gave. It's for when you don't know whether they'll give one.
        """
        example title="Did it give a value?" """
        SHOW RUNRESULT [3 * 4]
        SHOW RUNRESULT [FD 10]
        """
        see "run"
      }
      repeat {
        eg "REPEAT num instructionlist"
        desc "command. Runs the instructionlist repeatedly, num times."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Runs a list of instructions a number of times. [REPCOUNT] says which time it is, counting from 1, and the repeat counters panel shows it as Kame goes.

        A `REPEAT` can go inside another one, for patterns of shapes.
        """
        example title="A square" """
        REPEAT 4 [FD 150 RT 90]
        """
        example title="A flower of squares" """
        REPEAT 12 [
          REPEAT 4 [FD 100 RT 90]
          RT 30
        ]
        """
        example title="Getting bigger" """
        REPEAT 40 [FD REPCOUNT * 5 RT 90]
        """
        see "repcount" "forever" "for"
      }
      forever {
        eg "FOREVER instructionlist"
        desc "command. Runs the \"instructionlist\" repeatedly, until something inside the instructionlist (such as STOP or THROW) makes it stop."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Runs a list of instructions again and again, until something stops it: [STOP] in a procedure, [THROW], an error, or the Stop button. [REPCOUNT] says which time it is.
        """
        example title="Until it's big enough" """
        TO GROW
          FOREVER [
            IF REPCOUNT > 50 [STOP]
            FD REPCOUNT * 4 RT 121
          ]
        END
        GROW
        """
        see "repeat" "stop" "while"
      }
      repcount {
        eg "REPCOUNT"
        names "#"
        desc """
        outputs the repetition count of the innermost current REPEAT or FOREVER, starting from 1. If no REPEAT or FOREVER is active, outputs –1.

        The abbreviation # can be used for REPCOUNT unless the REPEAT is inside the template input to a higher order procedure such as FOREACH, in which case # has a different meaning.
        """
        differs "Outside REPEAT and FOREVER, REPCOUNT is an error rather than outputting -1, so a mistake shows up straight away. Inside FOREACH, MAP and the other template commands, it's the position of the member in the slot, like #."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Outputs which time round the innermost [REPEAT] or [FOREVER] is on, counting from 1. `#` is short for it.

        Inside [FOREACH], [MAP] and the other template commands, it's the position of the member in the slot. Outside all of these, it's an error.
        """
        example title="Counting the times" """
        REPEAT 5 [PRINT REPCOUNT]
        """
        example title="A spiral" """
        REPEAT 60 [FD # * 3 RT 61]
        """
        see "repeat" "forever"
      }
      if {
        eg """
        IF tf instructionlist
        (IF tf instructionlist1 instructionlist2)
        """
        desc """
        command. If the first input has the value TRUE, then IF runs the second input. If the first input has the value FALSE, then IF does nothing. (If given a third input, IF acts like IFELSE, as described below.) It is an error if the first input is not either TRUE or FALSE.

        For compatibility with earlier versions of Logo, if an IF instruction is not enclosed in parentheses, but the first thing on the instruction line after the second input expression is a literal list (i.e., a list in square brackets), the IF is treated as if it were IFELSE, but a warning message is given. If this aberrant IF appears in a procedure body, the warning is given only the first time the procedure is invoked in each Logo session.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Runs a list of instructions only if a question's answer is `TRUE`. The question is usually a comparison, such as `:size > 100`.

        With a third input, in parentheses, it runs that list instead when the answer is `FALSE`, like [IFELSE].
        """
        example title="Only big ones" """
        REPEAT 10 [
          IF REPCOUNT > 5 [SETPC "orange]
          FD 100 BK 100 RT 36
        ]
        """
        example title="Stopping a procedure" """
        TO SHRINK :size
          IF :size < 5 [STOP]
          SQUARE :size
          SHRINK :size * 0.8
        END
        SHRINK 300
        """
        see "ifelse" "stop" "and"
      }
      ifelse {
        eg "IFELSE tf instructionlist1 instructionlist2"
        desc "command or operation. If the first input has the value TRUE, then IFELSE runs the second input. If the first input has the value FALSE, then IFELSE runs the third input. IFELSE outputs a value if the instructionlist contains an expression that outputs a value."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Runs the first list of instructions if a question's answer is `TRUE`, and the second if it's `FALSE`. If the lists work out a value, `IFELSE` outputs it, so it can choose a value too.
        """
        example title="Two colours" """
        REPEAT 12 [
          IFELSE (REMAINDER REPCOUNT 2) = 0 [SETPC "gold] [SETPC "tomato]
          FD 150 BK 150 RT 30
        ]
        """
        example title="Choosing a value" """
        MAKE "n 7
        PRINT IFELSE :n > 5 ["big] ["small]
        """
        see "if" "case" "cond"
      }
      test {
        eg "TEST tf"
        desc "command. Remembers its input, which must be TRUE or FALSE, for use by later IFTRUE or IFFALSE instructions. The effect of TEST is local to the procedure in which it is used; any corresponding IFTRUE or IFFALSE must be in the same procedure or a subprocedure."
        see "iffalse"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it keeps a hidden answer for IFTRUE and IFFALSE to use later, which is confusing. Use IFELSE."
      }
      iftrue {
        eg """
        IFTRUE instructionlist
        IFT instructionlist
        """
        desc "command. Runs its input if the most recent TEST instruction had a TRUE input. The TEST must have been in the same procedure or a superprocedure."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it keeps a hidden answer for IFTRUE and IFFALSE to use later, which is confusing. Use IFELSE."
      }
      iffalse {
        eg """
        IFFALSE instructionlist
        IFF instructionlist
        """
        desc "command. Runs its input if the most recent TEST instruction had a FALSE input. The TEST must have been in the same procedure or a superprocedure."
        see "test"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it keeps a hidden answer for IFTRUE and IFFALSE to use later, which is confusing. Use IFELSE."
      }
      stop {
        eg "STOP"
        desc "command. Ends the running of the procedure in which it appears. Control is returned to the context in which that procedure was invoked. The stopped procedure does not output a value."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Stops the procedure it's in, and carries on after the instruction that called it. Outside a procedure, it's an error.

        It's how a procedure that calls itself knows when to finish.
        """
        example title="Counting down" """
        TO COUNTDOWN :n
          IF :n = 0 [PRINT "Liftoff! STOP]
          PRINT :n
          COUNTDOWN :n - 1
        END
        COUNTDOWN 3
        """
        see "output" "if" "forever"
      }
      output {
        eg """
        OUTPUT value
        OP value
        """
        desc "command. Ends the running of the procedure in which it appears. That procedure outputs the value value to the context in which it was invoked. Don’t be confused: OUTPUT itself is a command, but the procedure that invokes OUTPUT is an operation."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Stops the procedure it's in and gives back a value, to whatever called it. A procedure that outputs can be an input to other commands, as `SQRT` and `SUM` are. Outside a procedure, it's an error.
        """
        example title="A procedure that works something out" """
        TO AREA :width :height
          OUTPUT :width * :height
        END
        PRINT AREA 6 7
        """
        example title="Choosing a colour" """
        TO SHADE :n
          IF :n < 4 [OUTPUT "skyblue]
          OUTPUT "navy
        END
        SETPENSIZE 10
        REPEAT 8 [SETPC SHADE REPCOUNT FD 80 RT 45]
        """
        see "stop" "to"
      }
      catch {
        eg "CATCH tag instructionlist"
        desc """
        command or operation. Runs its second input. Outputs if that instructionlist outputs. If, while running the instructionlist, a THROW instruction is executed with a tag equal to the first input (case-insensitive comparison), then the running of the instructionlist is terminated immediately. In this case the CATCH outputs if a value input is given to THROW. The tag must be a word.

        If the tag is the word ERROR, then any error condition that arises during the running of the instructionlist has the effect of THROW "ERROR instead of printing an error message and returning to toplevel. The CATCH does not output if an error is caught. Also, during the running of the instructionlist, the variable ERRACT is temporarily unbound. (If there is an error while ERRACT has a value, that value is taken as an instructionlist to be run after printing the error message. Typically the value of ERRACT, if any, is the list [PAUSE].)
        """
        differs "Stop, from the toolbar, is never caught, even by CATCH \"ERROR."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Runs a list of instructions, and catches a [THROW] with the same tag, which stops the list there. If the throw has a value, `CATCH` outputs it.

        With the tag `"ERROR`, it catches errors instead, so the program carries on. [ERROR] then says what went wrong. The Stop button is never caught.
        """
        example title="Leaving early" """
        CATCH "done [
          REPEAT 100 [
            FD 10 RT 10
            IF REPCOUNT = 20 [THROW "done]
          ]
        ]
        PRINT [Stopped after 20]
        """
        example title="Carrying on after an error" """
        CATCH "ERROR [PRINT 10 / 0]
        PRINT [Still going]
        SHOW ERROR
        """
        see "throw" "error"
      }
      throw {
        eg """
        THROW tag
        (THROW tag value)
        """
        desc """
        command. Must be used within the scope of a CATCH with an equal tag. Ends the running of the instructionlist of the CATCH. If THROW is used with only one input, the corresponding CATCH does not output a value. If THROW is used with two inputs, the second provides an output for the CATCH.

        THROW "TOPLEVEL can be used to terminate all running procedures and interactive pauses, and return to the toplevel instruction prompt. Typing the system interrupt character (alt-S for wxWidgets; otherwise normally control-C for Unix, control-Q for DOS, or command-period for Mac) has the same effect.

        THROW "ERROR can be used to generate an error condition. If the error is not caught, it prints a message (THROW "ERROR) with the usual indication of where the error (in this case the THROW) occurred. If a second input is used along with a tag of ERROR, that second input is used as the text of the error message instead of the standard message. Also, in this case, the location indicated for the error will be, not the location of the THROW, but the location where the procedure containing the THROW was invoked. This allows user-defined procedures to generate error messages as if they were primitives. Note: in this case the corresponding CATCH "ERROR, if any, does not output, since the second input to THROW is not considered a return value.

        THROW "SYSTEM immediately leaves Logo, returning to the operating system, without printing the usual parting message and without deleting any editor temporary file written by EDIT.
        """
        differs "If no CATCH has the tag, it's an error: \"Can't find a CATCH for TAG\". THROW \"SYSTEM isn't special, since Pendown is a window rather than a terminal session."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Stops the instructions inside the [CATCH] with the same tag, and goes on after it. With a second input, in parentheses, the `CATCH` outputs that value. If no `CATCH` has the tag, it's an error.

        `THROW "ERROR` makes an error of your own, with the second input as its message.
        """
        example title="Throwing a value" """
        PRINT CATCH "found [
          FOREACH [3 8 12 5] [IF ? > 10 [(THROW "found ?)]]
          "none
        ]
        """
        example title="An error of your own" """
        TO CHECK :n
          IF :n < 0 [(THROW "ERROR [The size can't be negative])]
          OUTPUT :n
        END
        CATCH "ERROR [PRINT CHECK -5]
        PRINT FIRST BUTFIRST ERROR
        """
        see "catch"
      }
      error {
        eg "ERROR"
        desc "outputs a list describing the error just caught, if any. If there was not an error caught since the last use of ERROR, the empty list will be output. The error list contains four members: an integer code corresponding to the type of error, the text of the error message (as a single word including spaces), the name of the procedure in which the error occurred, and the instruction line on which the error occurred."
        differs "The error code, the first member, is always 0: Pendown's errors have messages rather than numbers. The last member is the number of the line the error happened on, or [] if there isn't one."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a list about the last error a [CATCH] `"ERROR` caught, then forgets it, so asking again gives an empty list.

        The list has four members: 0, the message, the procedure the error was in, and the line it was on, or `[]` where there's none.
        """
        example title="What went wrong" """
        CATCH "ERROR [FD "far]
        SHOW ERROR
        SHOW ERROR
        """
        see "catch" "erract"
      }
      pause {
        eg "PAUSE"
        desc """
        command or operation. Enters an interactive pause. The user is prompted for instructions, as at toplevel, but with a prompt that includes the name of the procedure in which PAUSE was invoked. Local variables of that procedure are available during the pause. PAUSE outputs if the pause is ended by a CONTINUE with an input.

        If the variable ERRACT exists, and an error condition occurs, the contents of that variable are run as an instructionlist. Typically ERRACT is given the value [PAUSE] so that an interactive pause will be entered in the event of an error. This allows the user to check values of local variables at the time of the error.

        Typing the system quit character (alt-S for wxWidgets; otherwise normally control-\\ for Unix, control-W for DOS, or command-comma for Mac) will also enter a pause.
        """
        see "erract"
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it stops for typing in the middle of a procedure. Use the Pause button, and the variables panel."
      }
      continue {
        eg """
        CONTINUE value
        CO value
        (CONTINUE)
        (CO)
        """
        desc """
        command. Ends the current interactive pause, returning to the context of the PAUSE invocation that began it. If CONTINUE is given an input, that value is used as the output from the PAUSE. If not, the PAUSE does not output.

        Exceptionally, the CONTINUE command can be used without its default input and without parentheses provided that nothing follows it on the instruction line.
        """
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it ends a PAUSE, which Pendown doesn't have."
      }
      wait {
        eg "WAIT time"
        desc "command. Delays further execution for time 60ths of a second. Also causes any buffered characters destined for the terminal to be printed immediately. WAIT 0 can be used to achieve this buffer flushing without actually waiting."
        differs "The turtle waits at every speed, including Instant: the program is paused for that long, with WAIT highlighted. When a picture is drawn with pendown-cli, WAIT does nothing."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 1
        help """
        Pauses for a time, in sixtieths of a second, so `WAIT 60` waits a second. Kame waits at every speed, even Instant, with `WAIT` highlighted. When a picture is drawn with `pendown-cli`, it does nothing.
        """
        example title="A ticking clock" """
        REPEAT 12 [FD 100 BK 100 RT 30 WAIT 15]
        """
        see "setspeed"
      }
      bye {
        eg "BYE"
        desc "command. Exits from Logo; returns to the operating system."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it leaves Logo. Close the window instead."
      }
      .maybeoutput {
        eg ".MAYBEOUTPUT value  (special form)"
        desc """
        works like OUTPUT except that the expression that provides the input value might not, in fact, output a value, in which case the effect is like STOP. This is intended for use in control structure definitions, for cases in which you don’t know whether or not some expression produces a value. Example:

            to invoke :function [:inputs] 2
            .maybeoutput apply :function :inputs
            end

            ? (invoke "print "a "b "c)
            a b c
            ? print (invoke "word "a "b "c)
            abc

        This is an alternative to RUNRESULT. It’s fast and easy to use, at the cost of being an exception to Logo’s evaluation rules. (Ordinarily, it should be an error if the expression that’s supposed to provide an input to something doesn’t have a value.)
        """
        see "output" "stop" "runresult"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      goto {
        eg "GOTO word"
        desc "command. Looks for a TAG command with the same input in the same procedure, and continues running the procedure from the location of that TAG. It is meaningless to use GOTO outside of a procedure."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it jumps to a TAG. Use REPEAT, WHILE, procedures, STOP and OUTPUT instead."
      }
      tag {
        eg "TAG quoted.word"
        desc "command. Does nothing. The input must be a literal word following a quotation mark (\"), not the result of a computation. Tags are used by the GOTO command."
        supported #false
        obsolete #true
        phase #null
        obsolete_reason "it marks a place for GOTO, which Pendown doesn't have."
      }
      ignore {
        eg "IGNORE value  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Does nothing. Used when an expression is evaluated for a side effect and its actual value is unimportant."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Does nothing with its input. Use it when you want an operation's side effect but not its value.
        """
        example title="Taking off the top" """
        MAKE "stack [a b c]
        IGNORE POP "stack
        SHOW :stack
        """
      }
      ` {
        eg "` list  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        outputs a list equal to its input but with certain substitutions. If a member of the input list is the word ‘,’ (comma) then the following member should be an instructionlist that produces an output when run. That output value replaces the comma and the instructionlist. If a member of the input list is the word ‘,@’ (comma atsign) then the following member should be an instructionlist that outputs a list when run. The members of that list replace the ‘,@’ and the instructionlist. Example:

            show `[foo baz ,[bf [a b c]] garply ,@[bf [a b c]]]

        will print

            [foo baz [b c] garply b c]

        A word starting with ‘,’ or ‘,@’ is treated as if the rest of the word were a one-word list, e.g., ‘,:foo’ is equivalent to ‘,[:Foo]’.

        A word starting with ‘",’ (quote comma) or ‘:,’ (colon comma) becomes a word starting with ‘"’ or ‘:’ but with the result of running the substitution (or its first word, if the result is a list) replacing what comes after the comma.

        Backquotes can be nested. Substitution is done only for commas at the same depth as the backquote in which they are found:

            ? show `[a `[b ,[1+2] ,[foo ,[1+3] d] e] f]
            [a ` [b , [1+2] , [foo 4 d] e] f]

            ?make "name1 "x
            ?make "name2 "y
            ? show `[a `[b ,:,:name1 ,",:name2 d] e]
            [a ` [b , [:x] , ["y] d] e]
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      for {
        eg "FOR forcontrol instructionlist  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        command. The first input must be a list containing three or four members: (1) a word, which will be used as the name of a local variable; (2) a word or list that will be evaluated as by RUN to determine a number, the starting value of the variable; (3) a word or list that will be evaluated to determine a number, the limit value of the variable; (4) an optional word or list that will be evaluated to determine the step size. If the fourth member is missing, the step size will be 1 or –1 depending on whether the limit value is greater than or less than the starting value, respectively.

        The second input is an instructionlist. The effect of FOR is to run that instructionlist repeatedly, assigning a new value to the control variable (the one named by the first member of the forcontrol list) each time. First the starting value is assigned to the control variable. Then the value is compared to the limit value. FOR is complete when the sign of (current - limit) is the same as the sign of the step size. (If no explicit step size is provided, the instructionlist is always run at least once. An explicit step size can lead to a zero-trip FOR, e.g., FOR [I 1 0 1] ...). Otherwise, the instructionlist is run, then the step is added to the current value of the control variable and FOR returns to the comparison step.

            ? for [i 2 7 1.5] [print :i]
            2
            3.5
            5
            6.5
            ?
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Runs a list of instructions once for each value of a counting variable. The first input is a list: the variable's name, where it starts, and where it stops, with an optional step to count by. Without a step it counts by 1, up or down.

        The variable belongs to the `FOR`, so it doesn't change one of the same name outside.
        """
        example title="Counting up" """
        FOR [i 1 5] [PRINT :i]
        """
        example title="Bigger and bigger squares" """
        FOR [size 20 200 20] [SQUARE :size]
        """
        example title="Counting down by twos" """
        FOR [n 10 0 -2] [PRINT :n]
        """
        see "repeat" "iseq" "foreach"
      }
      do.while {
        eg "DO.WHILE instructionlist tfexpression  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Repeatedly evaluates the instructionlist as long as the evaluated remains TRUE. Evaluates the first input first, so the instructionlist is always run at least once. The tfexpression must be an expressionlist whose value when evaluated is TRUE or FALSE."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Runs a list of instructions, then keeps running it while a question, given as a list, is `TRUE`. It always runs at least once, because it asks after.
        """
        example title="At least once" """
        MAKE "size 5
        DO.WHILE [SQUARE :size MAKE "size :size * 1.4] [:size < 300]
        """
        see "while" "do.until"
      }
      while {
        eg "WHILE tfexpression instructionlist  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Repeatedly evaluates the instructionlist as long as the evaluated remains TRUE. Evaluates the first input first, so the instructionlist may never be run at all. The tfexpression must be an expressionlist whose value when evaluated is TRUE or FALSE."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Keeps running a list of instructions while a question, given as a list, is `TRUE`. It asks first, so if the answer is `FALSE` to start with, the instructions never run.
        """
        example title="While it fits" """
        MAKE "radius 10
        WHILE [:radius < 200] [CIRCLE :radius MAKE "radius :radius + 25]
        """
        see "until" "do.while" "repeat"
      }
      do.until {
        eg "DO.UNTIL instructionlist tfexpression  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Repeatedly evaluates the instructionlist as long as the evaluated remains FALSE. Evaluates the first input first, so the instructionlist is always run at least once. The tfexpression must be an expressionlist whose value when evaluated is TRUE or FALSE."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Runs a list of instructions, then keeps running it until a question, given as a list, is `TRUE`. It always runs at least once, because it asks after.
        """
        example title="Until it's back" """
        DO.UNTIL [FD 50 RT 50] [HEADING = 0]
        """
        see "until" "do.while"
      }
      until {
        eg "UNTIL tfexpression instructionlist  (library procedure)"
        desc "command. Repeatedly evaluates the instructionlist as long as the evaluated remains FALSE. Evaluates the first input first, so the instructionlist may never be run at all. The tfexpression must be an expressionlist whose value when evaluated is TRUE or FALSE."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Keeps running a list of instructions until a question, given as a list, is `TRUE`. It asks first, so if the answer is `TRUE` to start with, the instructions never run.
        """
        example title="Until it's far enough" """
        UNTIL [YCOR > 200] [FD 30 RT 90 FD 10 LT 90]
        """
        see "while" "do.until"
      }
      case {
        eg "CASE value clauses  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        command or operation. The second input is a list of lists (clauses); each clause is a list whose first element is either a list of values or the word ELSE and whose butfirst is a Logo expression or instruction. CASE examines the clauses in order. If a clause begins with the word ELSE (upper or lower case), then the butfirst of that clause is evaluated and CASE outputs its value, if any. If the first input to CASE is a member of the first element of a clause, then the butfirst of that clause is evaluated and CASE outputs its value, if any. If neither of these conditions is met, then CASE goes on to the next clause. If no clause is satisfied, CASE does nothing. Example:

            to vowelp :letter
            output case :letter [ [[a e i o u] "true] [else "false] ]
            end
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Chooses what to do by matching a value. The second input is a list of clauses, each a list: a list of values to match, then what to do or output. The first clause whose values include the value is the one used. A clause starting with `ELSE` matches anything.
        """
        example title="What kind of letter" """
        TO KIND :letter
          OUTPUT CASE :letter [[[a e i o u] "vowel] [ELSE "consonant]]
        END
        PRINT KIND "e
        PRINT KIND "k
        """
        see "cond" "ifelse"
      }
      cond {
        eg "COND clauses  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        command or operation. The input is a list of lists (clauses); each clause is a list whose first element is either an expression whose value is TRUE or FALSE, or the word ELSE, and whose butfirst is a Logo expression or instruction. COND examines the clauses in order. If a clause begins with the word ELSE (upper or lower case), then the butfirst of that clause is evaluated and CASE outputs its value, if any. Otherwise, the first element of the clause is evaluated; the resulting value must be TRUE or FALSE. If it’s TRUE, then the butfirst of that clause is evaluated and COND outputs its value, if any. If the value is FALSE, then COND goes on to the next clause. If no clause is satisfied, COND does nothing. Example:

            to evens :numbers       ; select even numbers from a list
            op cond [ [[emptyp :numbers] []]
                      [[evenp first :numbers]       ; assuming EVENP is defined
                       fput first :numbers evens butfirst :numbers]
                      [else evens butfirst :numbers] ]
            end
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Chooses what to do by asking questions in turn. The input is a list of clauses, each a list: a question in a list, then what to do or output. The first clause whose question is `TRUE` is the one used. A clause starting with `ELSE` is always used.
        """
        example title="Hot or cold" """
        TO FEEL :degrees
          OUTPUT COND [[[:degrees > 25] "hot] [[:degrees > 10] "warm] [ELSE "cold]]
        END
        PRINT FEEL 30
        PRINT FEEL 15
        PRINT FEEL 2
        """
        see "case" "ifelse"
      }
    }
    template-based-iteration {
      apply {
        eg "APPLY template inputlist"
        desc "command or operation. Runs the template, filling its slots with the members of inputlist. The number of members in inputlist must be an acceptable number of slots for template. It is illegal to apply the primitive TO as a template, but anything else is okay. APPLY outputs what template outputs, if anything."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Runs a template with the members of a list as its inputs, and outputs what it outputs, if anything. A template can be a procedure's name, a list with `?` slots, or a list starting with input names, as in `[[x y] :x * :y]`.
        """
        example title="Inputs from a list" """
        PRINT APPLY "SUM [1 2 3]
        PRINT APPLY [?1 * ?2] [6 7]
        APPLY [[side n] REPEAT :n [FD :side RT 360 / :n]] [80 6]
        """
        see "invoke" "map" "template-slots"
      }
      invoke {
        eg """
        INVOKE template input  (library procedure)
        (INVOKE template input1 input2 ...)
        """
        desc "command or operation. Exactly like APPLY except that the inputs are provided as separate expressions rather than in a list."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Runs a template with its other inputs as the template's inputs, like [APPLY] without the list. Put the instruction in parentheses for more than one input.
        """
        example title="Calling a template" """
        PRINT INVOKE [? * ?] 9
        PRINT (INVOKE "WORD "sun "flower)
        """
        see "apply"
      }
      foreach {
        eg """
        FOREACH data template  (library procedure)
        (FOREACH data1 data2 ... template)
        """
        desc """
        command. Evaluates the template list repeatedly, once for each member of the data list. If more than one data list are given, each of them must be the same length. (The data inputs can be words, in which case the template is evaluated once for each character.)

        In a template, the symbol ?REST represents the portion of the data input to the right of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then ?REST would be replaced by [C D E]. If multiple parallel slots are used, then (?REST 1) goes with ?1, etc.

        In a template, the symbol # represents the position in the data input of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then # would be replaced by 2.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Runs a template once for each member of a list, or each character of a word, with the member in the slot `?`. [REPCOUNT], or `#`, is its position.

        With several lists, in parentheses, it takes a member from each at a time, in `?1`, `?2` and so on.
        """
        example title="For each colour" """
        SETPENSIZE 6
        FOREACH [red orange yellow green blue] [SETPC ? FD 100 RT 72]
        """
        example title="Named inputs" """
        FOREACH [50 100 150 200] [[size] SQUARE :size]
        """
        example title="Two lists together" """
        (FOREACH [a b c] [1 2 3] [PRINT WORD ?1 ?2])
        """
        see "map" "for" "template-slots"
      }
      map {
        eg """
        MAP template data  (library procedure)
        (MAP template data1 data2 ...)
        """
        desc """
        outputs a word or list, depending on the type of the data input, of the same length as that data input. (If more than one data input are given, the output is of the same type as data1.) Each member of the output is the result of evaluating the template list, filling the slots with the corresponding member(s) of the data input(s). (All data inputs must be the same length.) In the case of a word output, the results of the template evaluation must be words, and they are concatenated with WORD.

        In a template, the symbol ?REST represents the portion of the data input to the right of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then ?REST would be replaced by [C D E]. If multiple parallel slots are used, then (?REST 1) goes with ?1, etc.

        In a template, the symbol # represents the position in the data input of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then # would be replaced by 2.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs a list of what a template outputs for each member of a list, with the member in the slot `?`. If the data is a word, it outputs a word.
        """
        example title="Doubling every number" """
        SHOW MAP [? * 2] [1 2 3 4]
        SHOW MAP "UPPERCASE [kame the turtle]
        """
        example title="Positions on a circle" """
        MAKE "points MAP [LIST 150 * COS ? * 45 150 * SIN ? * 45] ISEQ 0 7
        FOREACH :points [PU SETPOS ? PD CIRCLE 20]
        """
        see "map.se" "foreach" "filter"
      }
      map.se {
        eg """
        MAP.SE template data  (library procedure)
        (MAP.SE template data1 data2 ...)
        """
        desc """
        outputs a list formed by evaluating the template list repeatedly and concatenating the results using SENTENCE. That is, the members of the output are the members of the results of the evaluations. The output list might, therefore, be of a different length from that of the data input(s). (If the result of an evaluation is the empty list, it contributes nothing to the final output.) The data inputs may be words or lists.

        In a template, the symbol ?REST represents the portion of the data input to the right of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then ?REST would be replaced by [C D E]. If multiple parallel slots are used, then (?REST 1) goes with ?1, etc.

        In a template, the symbol # represents the position in the data input of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then # would be replaced by 2.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Like [MAP], but joins what the template outputs with [SENTENCE], so lists are joined into one list rather than nested.
        """
        example title="Joining the results" """
        SHOW MAP [LIST ? ?] [a b c]
        SHOW MAP.SE [LIST ? ?] [a b c]
        """
        see "map" "sentence"
      }
      filter {
        eg "FILTER tftemplate data  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        outputs a word or list, depending on the type of the data input, containing a subset of the members (for a list) or characters (for a word) of the input. The template is evaluated once for each member or character of the data, and it must produce a TRUE or FALSE value. If the value is TRUE, then the corresponding input constituent is included in the output.

            ? print filter "vowelp "elephant
            eea
            ?

        In a template, the symbol ?REST represents the portion of the data input to the right of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then ?REST would be replaced by [C D E].

        In a template, the symbol # represents the position in the data input of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then # would be replaced by 2.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the members of a list, or characters of a word, for which a template outputs `TRUE`.
        """
        example title="Keeping some" """
        SHOW FILTER [? > 3] [1 5 2 8 3 9]
        PRINT FILTER [MEMBERP ? "aeiou] "elephant
        """
        see "find" "map"
      }
      find {
        eg "FIND tftemplate data  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        outputs the first constituent of the data input (the first member of a list, or the first character of a word) for which the value produced by evaluating the template with that consituent in its slot is TRUE. If there is no such constituent, the empty list is output.

        In a template, the symbol ?REST represents the portion of the data input to the right of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then ?REST would be replaced by [C D E].

        In a template, the symbol # represents the position in the data input of the member currently being used as the ? slot-filler. That is, if the data input is [A B C D E] and the template is being evaluated with ? replaced by B, then # would be replaced by 2.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Outputs the first member of a list, or character of a word, for which a template outputs `TRUE`, or an empty list if there's none.
        """
        example title="The first big one" """
        PRINT FIND [? > 10] [3 8 12 5 20]
        SHOW FIND [? > 100] [3 8 12]
        """
        see "filter" "memberp"
      }
      reduce {
        eg "REDUCE template data  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        outputs the result of applying the template to accumulate the members of the data input. The template must be a two-slot function. Typically it is an associative function name like SUM. If the data input has only one constituent (member in a list or character in a word), the output is that consituent. Otherwise, the template is first applied with ?1 filled with the next-to-last consitient and ?2 with the last constituent. Then, if there are more constituents, the template is applied with ?1 filled with the next constituent to the left and ?2 with the result from the previous evaluation. This process continues until all constituents have been used. The data input may not be empty.

        Note: If the template is, like SUM, the name of a procedure that is capable of accepting arbitrarily many inputs, it is more efficient to use APPLY instead of REDUCE. The latter is good for associative procedures that have been written to accept exactly two inputs:

            to max :a :b
            output ifelse :a > :b [:a] [:b]
            end

            print reduce "max [...]

        Alternatively, REDUCE can be used to write MAX as a procedure that accepts any number of inputs, as SUM does:

            to max [:inputs] 2
            if emptyp :inputs ~
               [(throw "error [not enough inputs to max])]
            output reduce [ifelse ?1 > ?2 [?1] [?2]] :inputs
            end
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 2
        help """
        Combines the members of a list into one value, using a template that takes two inputs. It works from the right: `REDUCE "DIFFERENCE [10 3 2]` is `10 - (3 - 2)`.
        """
        example title="Adding up a list" """
        PRINT REDUCE "SUM [1 2 3 4 5]
        PRINT REDUCE [IFELSE ?1 > ?2 [?1] [?2]] [3 9 2 7]
        """
        see "map" "apply"
      }
      crossmap {
        eg """
        CROSSMAP template listlist  (library procedure)
        (CROSSMAP template data1 data2 ...)
        """
        desc """
        outputs a list containing the results of template evaluations. Each data list contributes to a slot in the template; the number of slots is equal to the number of data list inputs. As a special case, if only one data list input is given, that list is taken as a list of data lists, and each of its members contributes values to a slot. CROSSMAP differs from MAP in that instead of taking members from the data inputs in parallel, it takes all possible combinations of members of data inputs, which need not be the same length.

            ? show (crossmap [word ?1 ?2] [a b c] [1 2 3 4])
            [a1 a2 a3 a4 b1 b2 b3 b4 c1 c2 c3 c4]
            ?

        For compatibility with the version in the first edition of CSLS 1, CROSSMAP templates may use the notation :1 instead of ?1 to indicate slots.
        """
        see "map"
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      cascade {
        eg """
        CASCADE endtest template startvalue  (library procedure)
        (CASCADE endtest tmp1 sv1 tmp2 sv2 ...)
        (CASCADE endtest tmp1 sv1 tmp2 sv2 ... finaltemplate)
        """
        desc """
        outputs the result of applying a template (or several templates, as explained below) repeatedly, with a given value filling the slot the first time, and the result of each application filling the slot for the following application.

        In the simplest case, CASCADE has three inputs. The second input is a one-slot expression template. That template is evaluated some number of times (perhaps zero). On the first evaluation, the slot is filled with the third input; on subsequent evaluations, the slot is filled with the result of the previous evaluation. The number of evaluations is determined by the first input. This can be either a nonnegative integer, in which case the template is evaluated that many times, or a predicate expression template, in which case it is evaluated (with the same slot filler that will be used for the evaluation of the second input) repeatedly, and the CASCADE evaluation continues as long as the predicate value is FALSE. (In other words, the predicate template indicates the condition for stopping.)

        If the template is evaluated zero times, the output from CASCADE is the third (startvalue) input. Otherwise, the output is the value produced by the last template evaluation.

        CASCADE templates may include the symbol # to represent the number of times the template has been evaluated. This slot is filled with 1 for the first evaluation, 2 for the second, and so on.

            ? show cascade 5 [lput # ?] []
            [1 2 3 4 5]
            ? show cascade [vowelp first ?] [bf ?] "spring
            ing
            ? show cascade 5 [# * ?] 1
            120
            ?

        Several cascaded results can be computed in parallel by providing additional template-startvalue pairs as inputs to CASCADE. In this case, all templates (including the endtest template, if used) are multi-slot, with the number of slots equal to the number of pairs of inputs. In each round of evaluations, ?2, for example, represents the result of evaluating the second template in the previous round. If the total number of inputs (including the first endtest input) is odd, then the output from CASCADE is the final value of the first template. If the total number of inputs is even, then the last input is a template that is evaluated once, after the end test is satisfied, to determine the output from CASCADE.

            to fibonacci :n
            output (cascade :n [?1 + ?2] 1 [?1] 0)
            end

            to piglatin :word
            output (cascade [vowelp first ?] ~
                            [word bf ? first ?] ~
                            :word ~
                            [word ? "ay])
            end
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      cascade.2 {
        eg "CASCADE.2 endtest temp1 startval1 temp2 startval2  (library procedure)"
        desc "outputs the result of invoking CASCADE with the same inputs. The only difference is that the default number of inputs is five instead of three."
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
      transfer {
        eg "TRANSFER endtest template inbasket  (library procedure)"
        desc """
        outputs the result of repeated evaluation of the template. The template is evaluated once for each member of the list inbasket. TRANSFER maintains an outbasket that is initially the empty list. After each evaluation of the template, the resulting value becomes the new outbasket.

        In the template, the symbol ?IN represents the current member from the inbasket; the symbol ?OUT represents the entire current outbasket. Other slot symbols should not be used.

        If the first (endtest) input is an empty list, evaluation continues until all inbasket members have been used. If not, the first input must be a predicate expression template, and evaluation continues until either that template’s value is TRUE or the inbasket is used up.
        """
        supported #true
        obsolete #false
        phase 7
      }
    }
  }
  macros {
    .macro {
      eg """
      .MACRO procname :input1 :input2 ...  (special form)
      .DEFMACRO procname text
      """
      desc """
      A macro is a special kind of procedure whose output is evaluated as Logo instructions in the context of the macro’s caller. .MACRO is exactly like TO except that the new procedure becomes a macro; .DEFMACRO is exactly like DEFINE with the same exception.

      Macros are useful for inventing new control structures comparable to REPEAT, IF, and so on. Such control structures can almost, but not quite, be duplicated by ordinary Logo procedures. For example, here is an ordinary procedure version of REPEAT:

          to my.repeat :num :instructions
          if :num=0 [stop]
          run :instructions
          my.repeat :num-1 :instructions
          end

      This version works fine for most purposes, e.g.,

          my.repeat 5 [print "hello]

      But it doesn’t work if the instructions to be carried out include OUTPUT, STOP, or LOCAL. For example, consider this procedure:

          to example
          print [Guess my secret word.  You get three guesses.]
          repeat 3 [type "|?? | ~
                    if readword = "secret [pr "Right! stop]]
          print [Sorry, the word was "secret"!]
          end

      This procedure works as written, but if MY.REPEAT is used instead of REPEAT, it won’t work because the STOP will stop MY.REPEAT instead of stopping EXAMPLE as desired.

      The solution is to make MY.REPEAT a macro. Instead of actually carrying out the computation, a macro must return a list containing Logo instructions. The contents of that list are evaluated as if they appeared in place of the call to the macro. Here’s a macro version of REPEAT:

          .macro my.repeat :num :instructions
          if :num=0 [output []]
          output sentence :instructions ~
                          (list "my.repeat :num-1 :instructions)
          end

      Every macro is an operation — it must always output something. Even in the base case, MY.REPEAT outputs an empty instruction list. To show how MY.REPEAT works, let’s take the example

          my.repeat 5 [print "hello]

      For this example, MY.REPEAT will output the instruction list

          [print "hello my.repeat 4 [print "hello]]

      Logo then executes these instructions in place of the original invocation of MY.REPEAT; this prints hello once and invokes another repetition.

      The technique just shown, although fairly easy to understand, has the defect of slowness because each repetition has to construct an instruction list for evaluation. Another approach is to make MY.REPEAT a macro that works just like the non-macro version unless the instructions to be repeated include OUTPUT or STOP:

          .macro my.repeat :num :instructions
          catch "repeat.catchtag ~
                [op repeat.done runresult [repeat1 :num :instructions]]
          op []
          end

          to repeat1 :num :instructions
          if :num=0 [throw "repeat.catchtag]
          run :instructions
          .maybeoutput repeat1 :num-1 :instructions
          end

          to repeat.done :repeat.result
          if emptyp :repeat.result [op [stop]]
          op list "output quoted first :repeat.result
          end

      If the instructions do not include STOP or OUTPUT, then REPEAT1 will reach its base case and invoke THROW. As a result, MY.REPEAT’s last instruction line will output an empty list, so the evaluation of the macro result by the caller will do nothing. But if a STOP or OUTPUT happens, then REPEAT.DONE will output a STOP or OUTPUT instruction that will be executed in the caller’s context.

      The macro-defining commands have names starting with a dot because macros are an advanced feature of Logo; it’s easy to get in trouble by defining a macro that doesn’t terminate, or by failing to construct the instruction list properly.

      Lisp users should note that Logo macros are not special forms. That is, the inputs to the macro are evaluated normally, as they would be for any other Logo procedure. It’s only the output from the macro that’s handled unusually.

      Here’s another example:

          .macro localmake :name :value
          output (list "local          ~
                       word "" :name   ~
                       "apply          ~
                       ""make          ~
                       (list :name :value))
          end

      It’s used this way:

          to try
          localmake "garply "hello
          print :garply
          end

      LOCALMAKE outputs the list

          [local "garply apply "make [garply hello]]

      The reason for the use of APPLY is to avoid having to decide whether or not the second input to MAKE requires a quotation mark before it. (In this case it would — MAKE "GARPLY "HELLO — but the quotation mark would be wrong if the value were a list.)

      It’s often convenient to use the ` function to construct the instruction list:

          .macro localmake :name :value
          op `[local ,[word "" :name] apply "make [,[:name] ,[:value]]]
          end

      On the other hand, ` is pretty slow, since it’s tree recursive and written in Logo.
      """
      see "to" "define" "apply" "stop" "output"
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 7
    }
    .defmacro {
      see ".macro"
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 7
    }
    macrop {
      eg """
      MACROP name
      MACRO? name
      """
      desc "outputs TRUE if its input is the name of a macro."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 7
    }
    macroexpand {
      eg "MACROEXPAND expr  (library procedure)"
      desc """
      takes as its input a Logo expression that invokes a macro (that is, one that begins with the name of a macro) and outputs the the Logo expression into which the macro would translate the input expression.

          .macro localmake :name :value
          op `[local ,[word "" :name] apply "make [,[:name] ,[:value]]]
          end

          ? show macroexpand [localmake "pi 3.14159]
          [local "pi apply "make [pi 3.14159]]
      """
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 7
    }
  }
  special-variables {
    allowgetset {
      eg "ALLOWGETSET  (variable)"
      desc "if TRUE, indicates that an attempt to use a procedure that doesn’t exist should be taken as an implicit getter or setter procedure (setter if the first three letters of the name are SET) for a variable of the same name (without the SET if appropriate)."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 7
    }
    buttonact {
      eg "BUTTONACT  (variable)"
      desc """
      if nonempty, should be an instruction list that will be evaluated whenever a mouse button is pressed. Note that the user may have released the button before the instructions are evaluated. BUTTON will still output which button was most recently pressed. CLICKPOS will output the position of the mouse cursor at the moment the button was pressed; this may be different from MOUSEPOS if the user moves the mouse after clicking.

      Note that it’s possible for the user to press a button during the evaluation of the instruction list. If this would confuse your program, prevent it by temporarily setting BUTTONACT to the empty list. One easy way to do that is the following:

          make "buttonact [button.action]

          to button.action [:buttonact []]
          ... ; whatever you want the button to do
          end
      """
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 6
    }
    caseignoredp {
      eg "CASEIGNOREDP  (variable)"
      desc "if TRUE, indicates that lower case and upper case letters should be considered equal by EQUALP, BEFOREP, MEMBERP, etc. Logo initially makes this variable TRUE, and buries it."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 2
      help """
      A special variable. While it's `TRUE`, as it is to start with, upper and lower case letters count as the same when words are compared, by `=`, [MEMBERP], [BEFOREP] and the rest. Make it `"false` to tell them apart.
      """
      example title="Telling cases apart" """
      PRINT "Kame = "kame
      MAKE "caseignoredp "false
      PRINT "Kame = "kame
      """
      see "equalp"
    }
    commandline {
      eg "COMMANDLINE  (variable)"
      desc "contains any text appearing after a hyphen on the command line used to start Logo."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 7
    }
    erract {
      eg "ERRACT  (variable)"
      desc "an instructionlist that will be run in the event of an error. Typically has the value [PAUSE] to allow interactive debugging."
      differs "There's no PAUSE, so ERRACT is for printing what went wrong: when an error isn't caught, the list runs where the error happened, so it can see the local variables there. Then the error stops the program as usual."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 2
      help """
      A special variable. Give it a list of instructions, and when an error isn't caught, they run where the error happened, before the program stops. They can see the local variables there, so they can print what went wrong.
      """
      example fails=#true title="Printing a clue" """
      MAKE "erract [PRINT SENTENCE [The size was] :size]
      TO BOX :size
        FD 100 / :size
      END
      BOX 0
      """
      see "catch" "error"
    }
    fullprintp {
      eg "FULLPRINTP  (variable)"
      desc "if TRUE, then words that were created using backslash or vertical bar (to include characters that would otherwise not be treated as part of a word) are printed with the backslashes or vertical bars shown, so that the printed result could be re-read by Logo to produce the same value. If FULLPRINTP is TRUE then the empty word (however it was created) prints as ||. (Otherwise it prints as nothing at all.)"
      differs "Pendown doesn't support backslashes or vertical bars in words yet, so FULLPRINTP only makes the empty word print as ||."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 2
      help """
      A special variable. While it's `TRUE`, the empty word prints as `||`, so you can see it's there. It's `FALSE` to start with.
      """
      example title="Seeing the empty word" """
      SHOW LIST "a "
      MAKE "fullprintp "true
      SHOW LIST "a "
      """
    }
    keyact {
      eg "KEYACT  (variable)"
      desc """
      if nonempty, should be an instruction list that will be evaluated whenever a key is pressed on the keyboard. The instruction list can use READCHAR to find out what key was pressed. Note that only keys that produce characters qualify; pressing SHIFT or CONTROL alone will not cause KEYACT to be evaluated.

      Note that it’s possible for the user to press a key during the evaluation of the instruction list. If this would confuse your program, prevent it by temporarily setting KEYACT to the empty list. One easy way to do that is the following:

          make "keyact [key.action]

          to key.action [:keyact []]
          ... ; whatever you want the key to do
          end
      """
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 6
    }
    loadnoisily {
      eg "LOADNOISILY  (variable)"
      desc "if TRUE, prints the names of procedures defined when loading from a file (including the temporary file made by EDIT)."
      see "edit"
      supported #false
      obsolete #true
      phase #null
      obsolete_reason "it controls what LOAD prints, and Pendown doesn't have LOAD."
    }
    printdepthlimit {
      eg "PRINTDEPTHLIMIT  (variable)"
      desc "if a nonnegative integer, indicates the maximum depth of sublist structure that will be printed by PRINT, etc."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 2
      help """
      A special variable. Give it a number, and [PRINT], [SHOW] and the rest print lists only that many levels deep, with `...` for anything deeper. Without it, they print everything.
      """
      example title="Only so deep" """
      MAKE "printdepthlimit 2
      SHOW [1 [2 [3 [4]]]]
      """
      see "printwidthlimit"
    }
    printwidthlimit {
      eg "PRINTWIDTHLIMIT  (variable)"
      desc "if a nonnegative integer, indicates the maximum number of members in any one list that will be printed by PRINT, etc."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 2
      help """
      A special variable. Give it a number, and [PRINT], [SHOW] and the rest print only that many members of each list, with `...` for the rest. Without it, they print everything.
      """
      example title="Only so many" """
      MAKE "printwidthlimit 5
      SHOW ISEQ 1 100
      """
      see "printdepthlimit"
    }
    redefp {
      eg "REDEFP  (variable)"
      desc "if TRUE, allows primitives to be erased (ERASE) or redefined (COPYDEF)."
      see "erase" "copydef"
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 7
    }
    startup {
      eg "STARTUP  (variable)"
      desc "if assigned a list value in a file loaded by LOAD, that value is run as an instructionlist after the loading."
      see "load"
      supported #false
      obsolete #true
      phase #null
      obsolete_reason "it runs after LOAD, and Pendown doesn't have LOAD."
    }
    unburyonedit {
      eg "UNBURYONEDIT  (variable)"
      desc "if TRUE, causes any procedure defined during EDIT or LOAD to be unburied, so that it will be saved by a later SAVE. Files that want to define and bury procedures must do it in that order."
      see "edit" "load" "save"
      supported #false
      obsolete #true
      phase #null
      obsolete_reason "it controls burying and EDIT, which Pendown doesn't have."
    }
    usealternatenames {
      eg "USEALTERNATENAMES  (variable)"
      desc """
      if TRUE, causes Logo to generate non-English words (from the Messages file) instead of TRUE, FALSE, END, etc.

      Logo provides the following buried variables that can be used by programs:
      """
      supported #false
      obsolete #true
      phase #null
      obsolete_reason "it swaps TRUE, FALSE and END for words in another language."
    }
    logoversion {
      eg "LOGOVERSION  (variable)"
      desc "a real number indicating the Logo version number, e.g., 5.5"
      differs "Pendown's version, such as 0.1, not UCBLogo's."
      supported #true
      obsolete #false
      phase 2
      help """
      A special variable: Pendown's version number.
      """
      example title="Which version?" """
      PRINT :logoversion
      """
    }
    logoplatform {
      eg "LOGOPLATFORM  (variable)"
      desc "one of the following words: wxWidgets, X11, Windows, or Unix-nographics."
      supported #false
      obsolete #true
      phase #null
      obsolete_reason "it tells a program which build of UCBLogo it's in, and Pendown has only one."
    }
  }
}
